/* ============================================================
   Glossary term data — window.GLOSSARY
   Single source of truth for the glossary AND the flashcards.
   Each entry: { t: term, d: definition, s?: "course/slug", l?: lesson label }.
   Loaded by glossary.html and flashcards.html; also read by
   tools/build-search-index.py. Add a term here, not in the HTML.
   ============================================================ */
window.GLOSSARY = [
      { t: "Instrumental variable", d: "A variable that moves your endogenous regressor, is as good as randomly assigned, and reaches the outcome by no other route. Those three conditions are relevance, independence and exclusion, and only the first can be checked in the data.", s: "stats-3/instrumental-variables", l: "Instrumental Variables & 2SLS" },
      { t: "Endogeneity", d: "The condition that a regressor is correlated with the error term, so its coefficient cannot be read as a causal effect. It arises in three ways: an omitted common cause, reverse causality, and measurement error in the regressor itself. It is the failure of exogeneity.", s: "stats-3/instrumental-variables", l: "Instrumental Variables & 2SLS" },
      { t: "Exclusion restriction", d: "The assumption that an instrument affects the outcome only through the regressor it moves. With a single instrument no test can check it, so it has to be argued from where the instrument came from.", s: "stats-3/instrumental-variables", l: "Instrumental Variables & 2SLS" },
      { t: "First stage", d: "The regression of the endogenous regressor on the instrument. Its slope is the change in the regressor per unit of the instrument. Its F statistic measures relevance, the only one of the three instrumental-variable conditions the data can check.", s: "stats-3/instrumental-variables", l: "Instrumental Variables & 2SLS" },
      { t: "Reduced form", d: "The regression of the outcome directly on the instrument, skipping the regressor entirely. Dividing it by the first-stage slope gives the causal effect, so an instrument with no effect on the outcome gives an estimate of zero, however strong the first stage.", s: "stats-3/instrumental-variables", l: "Instrumental Variables & 2SLS" },
      { t: "Wald ratio", d: "The instrumental-variable estimate in its simplest form: the effect of the instrument on the outcome divided by the effect of the instrument on the regressor. With one instrument and one endogenous regressor it equals the two-stage least squares estimate exactly.", s: "stats-3/instrumental-variables", l: "Instrumental Variables & 2SLS" },
      { t: "Two-stage least squares (2SLS)", d: "Fit the regressor on the instruments, keep the predicted values, then regress the outcome on those predictions. It gives the Wald ratio when there is one instrument and generalizes to several instruments and added controls. Running the two regressions by hand gives the right coefficient and the wrong standard error.", s: "stats-3/instrumental-variables", l: "Instrumental Variables & 2SLS" },
      { t: "Weak instrument", d: "An instrument only weakly related to the regressor, so the estimate divides by a first-stage slope near zero. The estimate is then very imprecise and biased toward the ordinary least squares estimate. The usual rule of thumb flags a first-stage F below about 10.", s: "stats-3/instrumental-variables", l: "Instrumental Variables & 2SLS" },
      { t: "Local average treatment effect (LATE)", d: "What an instrumental-variable estimate actually recovers when the effect varies across people: the average effect among compliers, the people whose regressor the instrument moved. It is not the population average, and a results section should say so.", s: "stats-3/instrumental-variables", l: "Instrumental Variables & 2SLS" },
      { t: "Complier", d: "Someone whose regressor follows the instrument: treated when the instrument says so, untreated when it does not. Contrast always-takers and never-takers, whose behavior the instrument cannot change and who therefore contribute nothing to the estimate.", s: "stats-3/instrumental-variables", l: "Instrumental Variables & 2SLS" },
      { t: "Running variable", d: "The continuous score a cutoff rule is applied to: an exam mark, a household income, a birth date. Center it at the cutoff so that the treatment coefficient is the jump at the threshold. Uncentered, with separate slopes on each side, that coefficient is the gap at a score of zero, which may be far from any real data.", s: "stats-3/regression-discontinuity", l: "Regression Discontinuity Design" },
      { t: "Bandwidth", d: "The width of the window on either side of the cutoff that a regression discontinuity is fitted in. A narrow window is noisier but depends less on the assumed curve; a wide one is more precise but depends more on it. Estimates are usually reported for several bandwidths.", s: "stats-3/regression-discontinuity", l: "Regression Discontinuity Design" },
      { t: "Sharp regression discontinuity", d: "The case where the cutoff rule is obeyed exactly, so being above the threshold and being treated are the same thing and the jump in the outcome is the effect.", s: "stats-3/regression-discontinuity", l: "Regression Discontinuity Design" },
      { t: "Fuzzy regression discontinuity", d: "The case where crossing the cutoff only raises the probability of treatment. The jump in the outcome divided by the jump in treatment probability is the estimate, which makes the design an instrumental-variables analysis with the cutoff as the instrument.", s: "stats-3/regression-discontinuity", l: "Regression Discontinuity Design" },
      { t: "McCrary density test", d: "A check on whether people sorted themselves across a cutoff. It estimates the density of the running variable separately on each side and tests whether the two meet at the threshold; a shortage below and a pile-up above means the score could be manipulated.", s: "stats-3/regression-discontinuity", l: "Regression Discontinuity Design" },
      { t: "Parallel trends", d: "The assumption difference-in-differences rests on: absent the treatment, the two groups would have moved by the same amount. It allows any standing gap between them and forbids a difference in slope. It describes a path that was never observed, so no test can check it directly.", s: "stats-3/difference-in-differences", l: "Difference-in-Differences" },
      { t: "Event study", d: "A difference-in-differences run one period at a time, each coefficient measured against a reference period just before the treatment. The coefficients before the treatment should be close to zero. That is the closest the data can come to a test of parallel trends.", s: "stats-3/difference-in-differences", l: "Difference-in-Differences" },
      { t: "Two-way fixed effects", d: "A panel regression carrying a dummy for every unit and a dummy for every period, so anything fixed about a unit and anything that hit every unit at once is absorbed. With two groups and two periods it returns exactly the difference-in-differences estimate.", s: "stats-3/difference-in-differences", l: "Difference-in-Differences" },
      { t: "Staggered adoption", d: "A panel in which units start treatment at different dates. The ordinary two-way fixed-effects regression can be badly biased here, because it uses already-treated units as comparisons for later-treated ones. Modern estimators compare each adoption cohort with units not yet treated.", s: "stats-3/difference-in-differences", l: "Difference-in-Differences" },
      { t: "Clustered standard errors", d: "Standard errors that treat a whole group of correlated observations as one draw rather than many. Repeated readings of the same school or person are not independent, so standard errors that ignore the grouping are too small. Cluster at the level at which the treatment was assigned.", s: "stats-3/difference-in-differences", l: "Difference-in-Differences" },
      { t: "Polynomial term", d: "An added x² (or higher power) that lets a fitted curve turn around. The turning point is at x = −b₁/(2b₂), and neither coefficient is interpretable on its own.", s: "stats-2/nonlinear-relationships-and-transformations", l: "Non-Linear Relationships & Transformations" },
      { t: "Retransformation bias", d: "Exponentiating the fitted value of a log-outcome model estimates the median of y, not its mean, so a naive back-transform runs low.", s: "stats-2/nonlinear-relationships-and-transformations", l: "Non-Linear Relationships & Transformations" },
      { t: "Curvature (in residuals)", d: "An arch or valley in a plot of residuals against fitted values. It shows that the relationship bends where the model assumed a straight line.", s: "stats-2/nonlinear-relationships-and-transformations", l: "Non-Linear Relationships & Transformations" },
      { t: "Covariance", d: "The average product of paired deviations, Σ(x − x̄)(y − ȳ)/(n − 1). Divide it by both standard deviations for the correlation, or by var(x) alone for the regression slope.", s: "stats-2/inference-for-regression", l: "Inference for Regression" },
      { t: "Exogeneity", d: "The condition that a predictor is unrelated to the error term. No residual plot can check it, because residuals are built to be uncorrelated with the predictors whether it holds or not.", s: "stats-2/inference-for-regression", l: "Inference for Regression" },
      { t: "BLUE", d: "Best linear unbiased estimate. Under linearity, independence, equal variance and exogeneity, least squares has the smallest standard error of any estimator that is linear in y and unbiased. Normality is not required for it.", s: "stats-2/inference-for-regression", l: "Inference for Regression" },
      { t: "Population regression model", d: "y = β₀ + β₁x + ε with ε normal, mean zero and the same spread at every x. The model that makes inference about a slope possible.", s: "stats-2/inference-for-regression", l: "Inference for Regression" },
      { t: "Residual standard error (s)", d: "The typical size of a residual, the square root of SSE/(n−2). It estimates σ, the scatter around the population line.", s: "stats-2/inference-for-regression", l: "Inference for Regression" },
      { t: "Standard error of the slope", d: "s divided by the square root of the sum of squared x deviations. Small scatter and widely spread predictor values both make it small.", s: "stats-2/inference-for-regression", l: "Inference for Regression" },
      { t: "Confidence band", d: "The band around a fitted line formed by a confidence interval for the MEAN response at each x. It is narrowest at the mean of x and widens toward the edges.", s: "stats-2/inference-for-regression", l: "Inference for Regression" },
      { t: "Prediction interval", d: "The interval for ONE new observation at a given x. Wider than the confidence interval for the mean, and it does not shrink toward zero as n grows.", s: "stats-2/inference-for-regression", l: "Inference for Regression" },
      { t: "Regression ANOVA table", d: "The decomposition SST = SSR + SSE printed above the coefficients. In simple regression its F equals the slope t squared.", s: "stats-2/inference-for-regression", l: "Inference for Regression" },
      { t: "Sample proportion (p̂)", d: "The fraction of successes in a sample, x/n. Its mean is p and its standard deviation is the square root of p(1−p)/n.", s: "stats-1/inference-for-proportions", l: "Inference for Proportions" },
      { t: "Pooled proportion", d: "The combined success rate across both groups, (x₁+x₂)/(n₁+n₂). Used in the standard error of a two-proportion z test, because H₀ says the two proportions are equal.", s: "stats-1/inference-for-proportions", l: "Inference for Proportions" },
      { t: "Success-failure condition", d: "Inference on a proportion needs at least 10 successes and at least 10 failures, so the normal approximation has room on both sides. It is a condition on both counts, not on n.", s: "stats-1/inference-for-proportions", l: "Inference for Proportions" },
      { t: "Two-proportion z test", d: "A test of whether two independent groups have the same population proportion. On a 2×2 table it is algebraically the chi-square test, with χ² equal to z squared.", s: "stats-1/inference-for-proportions", l: "Inference for Proportions" },
      { t: "Binomial distribution", d: "The distribution of the number of successes in n independent trials that each succeed with the same probability p. Written B(n, p).", s: "stats-1/binomial-distribution", l: "The Binomial Distribution" },
      { t: "Binomial coefficient", d: "C(n, k) = n! / (k!(n−k)!), the number of different orders in which k successes can occur among n trials.", s: "stats-1/binomial-distribution", l: "The Binomial Distribution" },
      { t: "Bernoulli trial", d: "A single trial with two outcomes and a fixed success probability. A binomial count is n of them added up.", s: "stats-1/binomial-distribution", l: "The Binomial Distribution" },
      { t: "Binomial setting", d: "The four conditions a count must meet to be binomial: a fixed n, two outcomes per trial, independent trials, and the same p every trial.", s: "stats-1/binomial-distribution", l: "The Binomial Distribution" },
      { t: "Simple random sample (SRS)", d: "A sample drawn so that every group of n members of the population is equally likely to be the one selected. The reference design all others are judged against.", s: "stats-1/producing-data-and-sampling-design", l: "Producing Data & Sampling Design" },
      { t: "Stratified sample", d: "A sample drawn by splitting the population into groups that differ from each other, then sampling at random inside each group. Removes between-stratum variation from the sampling error.", s: "stats-1/producing-data-and-sampling-design", l: "Producing Data & Sampling Design" },
      { t: "Convenience sample", d: "A sample of whoever is easiest to reach. Cheap, and biased by whatever made those people easy to reach.", s: "stats-1/producing-data-and-sampling-design", l: "Producing Data & Sampling Design" },
      { t: "Voluntary response sample", d: "A sample of people who put themselves forward. Those with the strongest opinions are the ones who answer, so the estimate is biased toward their views.", s: "stats-1/producing-data-and-sampling-design", l: "Producing Data & Sampling Design" },
      { t: "Undercoverage", d: "A flaw in the sampling frame: part of the population is left off the list, so those people could never have been selected.", s: "stats-1/producing-data-and-sampling-design", l: "Producing Data & Sampling Design" },
      { t: "Non-response", d: "Selected participants who do not take part. It matters when the people who decline differ from the people who reply.", s: "stats-1/producing-data-and-sampling-design", l: "Producing Data & Sampling Design" },
      { t: "Response bias", d: "A systematic distortion in the answers themselves, from a socially awkward question, an interviewer effect, or faulty recall.", s: "stats-1/producing-data-and-sampling-design", l: "Producing Data & Sampling Design" },
      { t: "Confounder", d: "A variable that differs between your groups alongside the one you care about, so it offers a second explanation for any difference in outcome.", s: "stats-1/producing-data-and-sampling-design", l: "Producing Data & Sampling Design" },
      { t: "Blocking", d: "Sorting participants into similar blocks and randomizing within each, so a known source of variation is spread evenly across conditions.", s: "stats-1/producing-data-and-sampling-design", l: "Producing Data & Sampling Design" },
      { t: "Alpha (significance level)", d: "The Type I error rate you accept in advance: the probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis. Conventionally 0.05.", s: "stats-1/hypothesis-testing-logic", l: "Hypothesis Testing Logic" },
      { t: "Alternative hypothesis (H₁)", d: "The claim you're gathering evidence for, that an effect, difference, or relationship exists.", s: "stats-1/hypothesis-testing-logic", l: "Hypothesis Testing Logic" },
      { t: "ANOVA", d: "Analysis of Variance — a test for whether three or more group means differ, by comparing variance between groups to variance within groups.", s: "stats-2/one-way-anova", l: "One-Way ANOVA" },
      { t: "ANCOVA", d: "Analysis of Covariance. Compares group means after statistically adjusting for one or more covariates, which corrects baseline imbalance and reduces error variance.", s: "stats-2/ancova", l: "ANCOVA: Controlling for Covariates" },
      { t: "Adjusted means", d: "Group means estimated at the same covariate value (usually the overall mean). These are the means ANCOVA compares.", s: "stats-2/ancova", l: "ANCOVA: Controlling for Covariates" },
      { t: "AIC / BIC", d: "Information criteria that score a model on fit while penalizing complexity, so you can compare non-nested models. Lower is better.", s: "stats-2/model-comparison", l: "Model Comparison" },
      { t: "Bayesian inference", d: "An approach that treats unknown parameters as having probability distributions, updating a prior belief with data to get a posterior.", s: "stats-3/bayesian-thinking", l: "Bayesian Thinking" },
      { t: "Between-groups variance", d: "How much the group means differ from the overall mean, the 'signal' in the numerator of the ANOVA F-ratio.", s: "stats-2/one-way-anova", l: "One-Way ANOVA" },
      { t: "Grand mean", d: "The mean of every observation pooled together, ignoring which group each came from. Between-group variation is measured from it, and within-group variation from each observation's own group mean. Using the wrong mean for either gives a wrong ANOVA table.", s: "stats-2/one-way-anova", l: "One-Way ANOVA" },
      { t: "Mean square (MS)", d: "A sum of squares divided by its degrees of freedom, which makes it a variance. An ANOVA table prints two that matter: MS between groups, the variation the grouping explains, and MS within groups, the variation it does not. Their ratio is F.", s: "stats-2/one-way-anova", l: "One-Way ANOVA" },
      { t: "Pooled standard deviation (sp)", d: "The square root of the within-groups mean square: one estimate of spread built from every group at once. Pairwise follow-ups to an ANOVA use it as their error term, so each comparison gets degrees of freedom from every group and not only from the two being compared. This is a reason to compute the full ANOVA table even when you already know which pairs interest you.", s: "stats-2/one-way-anova", l: "One-Way ANOVA" },
      { t: "Within-groups variance", d: "How far individual scores fall from their own group's mean, the 'noise' in the denominator of the F-ratio. Two observations in the same group were treated identically, so the design cannot explain this variation. It is therefore the baseline against which the group differences are judged.", s: "stats-2/one-way-anova", l: "One-Way ANOVA" },
      { t: "Bonferroni correction", d: "A conservative fix for multiple comparisons: divide your alpha by the number of tests to keep the overall error rate in check.", s: "stats-2/post-hoc-tests", l: "Post-Hoc Tests" },
      { t: "Planned contrast", d: "A comparison among group means chosen before the data existed and written as coefficients that sum to zero. Because a study tests only a few, each for a stated reason, they need little or no multiple-comparison correction, and a direction fixed in advance justifies a one-tailed test. The coefficients do not record when they were chosen, so only a dated analysis plan shows that a contrast was planned.", s: "stats-2/post-hoc-tests", l: "Post-Hoc Tests" },
      { t: "Contrast coefficient", d: "One of the weights in a contrast, the numbers that say which groups the question involves and on which side. They must sum to zero, so the contrast is a difference between groups and equals zero when all the means are equal. Multiplying every coefficient by the same number rescales the estimate and its confidence interval but leaves the t and the p-value unchanged.", s: "stats-2/post-hoc-tests", l: "Post-Hoc Tests" },
      { t: "Simultaneous confidence interval", d: "One of a family of intervals built on a corrected critical value, so the stated coverage holds for the whole set at once rather than for each interval on its own. After an ANOVA it is the pair's mean difference plus or minus the Bonferroni critical value times the pooled standard error. It contains zero exactly when the corrected test does not reject.", s: "stats-2/post-hoc-tests", l: "Post-Hoc Tests" },
      { t: "Bootstrap", d: "Estimating uncertainty by resampling your data with replacement thousands of times and recomputing the statistic each time. It needs no standard-error formula.", s: "stats-3/bootstrap-and-resampling", l: "Bootstrap & Resampling" },
      { t: "Categorical variable", d: "A variable whose values are labels or categories (e.g. condition, group) rather than measured quantities.", s: "stats-1/types-of-data", l: "Types of Data" },
      { t: "Central Limit Theorem", d: "The result that the distribution of the sample mean approaches normal as n grows, whatever the population's shape, provided the population has a finite variance.", s: "stats-1/central-limit-theorem", l: "Central Limit Theorem" },
      { t: "Sample space", d: "Every outcome a random phenomenon can produce, written S. Two coins give S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}, four outcomes rather than three, because HT and TH are different results that happen to share a count.", s: "stats-1/probability-basics", l: "Probability Basics" },
      { t: "Event", d: "Any collection of outcomes from the sample space. Rolling an even number on one die is the event {2, 4, 6}. When every outcome is equally likely, the probability of an event is simply how many outcomes it contains divided by how many the sample space contains.", s: "stats-1/probability-basics", l: "Probability Basics" },
      { t: "Complement rule", d: "P(not A) = 1 − P(A). It is most useful for 'at least one' questions: work out the probability that the event never happens, then subtract it from 1.", s: "stats-1/probability-basics", l: "Probability Basics" },
      { t: "Disjoint events", d: "Two events that cannot both happen, also called mutually exclusive. Their overlap is empty, so P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) with nothing to subtract. Disjoint is not the same as independent; disjoint events are maximally dependent, since one happening rules the other out.", s: "stats-1/probability-basics", l: "Probability Basics" },
      { t: "General addition rule", d: "P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). Adding the two probabilities counts the overlap twice, once inside each total, so one copy is subtracted. The disjoint version is the special case where the overlap is empty.", s: "stats-1/probability-basics", l: "Probability Basics" },
      { t: "Conditional probability", d: "P(A | B), the probability of A among the cases that are also B. Defined as P(A and B) / P(B): conditioning keeps only the cases in B, so the denominator is B's own total and not the whole sample.", s: "stats-1/probability-basics", l: "Probability Basics" },
      { t: "Independence", d: "Two events are independent when knowing that one happened leaves the probability of the other unchanged, so P(A | B) = P(A) and probabilities multiply. It is an assumption that has to be justified, and most real-world probability errors come from making it where it does not hold.", s: "stats-1/probability-basics", l: "Probability Basics" },
      { t: "General multiplication rule", d: "P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B | A), valid whether or not the events are independent. Dealing two aces without replacement is (4/52) × (3/51), because the second probability depends on what the first draw removed. Where the events are independent it reduces to P(A) × P(B).", s: "stats-1/probability-basics", l: "Probability Basics" },
      { t: "Bayes' rule", d: "P(A | B) = P(B | A) × P(A) / P(B), the arithmetic that turns a conditional probability around. A test's accuracy tells you P(positive | ill); a person holding a positive result wants P(ill | positive), and at a low prevalence the two can differ by a factor of ten.", s: "stats-1/probability-basics", l: "Probability Basics" },
      { t: "Law of total probability", d: "An event reachable along several disjoint routes has a probability equal to the sum of those routes: P(B) = P(B | A)P(A) + P(B | not A)P(not A). It gives the denominator in Bayes' rule. In a screening problem that denominator is the probability of any positive result, true or false.", s: "stats-1/probability-basics", l: "Probability Basics" },
      { t: "Random variable", d: "A rule attaching a number to the outcome of a random phenomenon, with the value settled only once the phenomenon runs. Written as a capital letter, with a small letter for a particular value, so P(X = 3) is the probability of the value 3. Discrete ones take values you can list; continuous ones take any value in an interval.", s: "stats-1/binomial-distribution", l: "The Binomial Distribution" },
      { t: "Expected value", d: "The mean of a random variable, μ = Σ x · p: each value weighted by its own probability. It need not be a value the variable can take, so a count of bikes per hour can have an expected value of 1.95. The matching spread is σ² = Σ (x − μ)² · p.", s: "stats-1/binomial-distribution", l: "The Binomial Distribution" },
      { t: "Triangular distribution", d: "The straight-sided, peaked distribution of the sum of two independent uniforms. The classic case is the total of two dice: one way to roll a 2, six ways to roll a 7. Adding a third uniform already rounds the sides toward a bell.", s: "stats-1/probability-basics", l: "Probability Basics" },
      { t: "Chi-square test", d: "A test for association between categorical variables, comparing observed counts to the counts expected under independence.", s: "stats-1/chi-square-tests", l: "Chi-Square Tests" },
      { t: "Cohen's d", d: "A standardized effect size for the difference between two means, expressed in standard-deviation units.", s: "stats-3/effect-size-and-power", l: "Effect Size & Power" },
      { t: "Confidence interval", d: "A range that, over many repeated samples, would contain the true parameter a stated percentage of the time (e.g. 95%).", s: "stats-1/confidence-intervals", l: "Confidence Intervals" },
      { t: "Confounding variable", d: "A lurking third variable that influences both the predictor and the outcome, distorting their apparent relationship.", s: "stats-3/causal-dags-and-confounding", l: "Causal DAGs & Confounding" },
      { t: "Covariate", d: "A continuous variable (like a pretest or age) included in a model to statistically control for its influence on the outcome.", s: "stats-2/ancova", l: "ANCOVA: Controlling for Covariates" },
      { t: "Correlation (Pearson r)", d: "A number from −1 to +1 measuring the strength and direction of a straight-line relationship between two variables.", s: "stats-1/correlation", l: "Correlation" },
      { t: "Standardized product", d: "The building block of Pearson's r: for one case, (x − x̄)/sx multiplied by (y − ȳ)/sy. It is positive when the case is on the same side of both means, negative when it is on opposite sides, and zero when the case is exactly average on either variable. Averaging these over n − 1 cases gives r.", s: "stats-1/correlation", l: "Correlation" },
      { t: "Cook's distance", d: "How far the whole fitted regression moves when one case is deleted, combining that case's leverage with the size of its residual: D = (r²/p) × h/(1 − h). Common flags are D above 1 or above 4/n. Check a flagged case before deciding what to do with it. The flag alone is no reason to delete it.", s: "stats-2/assumptions-of-regression", l: "Assumptions of Regression" },
      { t: "Credible interval", d: "The Bayesian counterpart of a confidence interval: a range that contains the parameter with a stated posterior probability.", s: "stats-3/bayesian-estimation", l: "Bayesian Estimation" },
      { t: "Censoring", d: "An observation whose event hadn't happened yet when observation stopped. You know only that the true time exceeds what you saw.", s: "stats-3/survival-analysis", l: "Survival Analysis" },
      { t: "Collider", d: "A variable caused by both X and Y (X → C ← Y). Harmless if left alone — but adjusting for it creates a spurious X–Y association.", s: "stats-3/causal-dags-and-confounding", l: "Causal DAGs & Confounding" },
      { t: "Criterion (response bias)", d: "In signal detection theory, the evidence cutoff above which an observer answers 'yes.' The statistic c measures its placement: 0 is unbiased, positive is conservative (few yeses), negative is liberal.", s: "stats-3/signal-detection-theory", l: "Signal Detection Theory" },
      { t: "Cross-validation", d: "Estimating out-of-sample accuracy by repeatedly fitting on part of the data and testing on the held-out rest (e.g. 5-fold).", s: "stats-3/cross-validation-and-overfitting", l: "Cross-Validation & Overfitting" },
      { t: "d′ (d-prime)", d: "Signal detection theory's bias-free sensitivity index: the distance between the means of the signal and noise evidence distributions, in SD units. Computed as z(hit rate) − z(false-alarm rate).", s: "stats-3/signal-detection-theory", l: "Signal Detection Theory" },
      { t: "DAG (directed acyclic graph)", d: "A diagram of causal assumptions, arrows from causes to effects, that determines which variables you should (and shouldn't) adjust for.", s: "stats-3/causal-dags-and-confounding", l: "Causal DAGs & Confounding" },
      { t: "Degrees of freedom", d: "The number of values free to vary when estimating a statistic. It sets the exact shape of t, F, and χ² distributions.", s: "stats-1/one-sample-and-paired-t-tests", l: "One-Sample & Paired t-Tests" },
      { t: "Descriptive statistics", d: "Summaries of the data you actually have — means, spreads, charts — making no claim beyond the sample.", s: "stats-1/what-is-statistics", l: "What Is Statistics?" },
      { t: "Deviance", d: "What a generalized linear model minimizes in place of the sum of squared residuals: twice the log-likelihood gap between your model and one that reproduces every observation exactly. For a well-specified Poisson model it should be close to its degrees of freedom, so a deviance/df ratio well above 1 signals overdispersion.", s: "stats-3/generalized-linear-models", l: "Generalized Linear Models" },
      { t: "Dummy coding", d: "Representing a categorical predictor as 0/1 indicator variables so it can enter a regression, relative to a reference category.", s: "stats-2/categorical-predictors-and-dummy-coding", l: "Categorical Predictors & Dummy Coding" },
      { t: "Effect size", d: "How big an effect is, independent of sample size: the practical significance that a p-value alone can't tell you.", s: "stats-3/effect-size-and-power", l: "Effect Size & Power" },
      { t: "Efficiency (of an estimator)", d: "How precise an estimator is for a given sample size: of two unbiased estimators, the more efficient one has the smaller standard error. On a normal population the sample median's standard error is √(π/2) ≈ 1.25 times the mean's, so matching the mean's precision with a median needs about 1.57 times as many participants.", s: "stats-1/sampling-distributions", l: "Sampling Distributions" },
      { t: "Eigenvalue", d: "In factor analysis and PCA, the variance one factor accounts for, equal to the sum of its squared loadings down the column. Each standardized item has a variance of 1, so an eigenvalue counts how many items' worth of variance the factor explains. The Kaiser criterion's threshold of 1 is therefore the variance of a single item.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Rotation", d: "Turning a factor solution to a position that is easier to interpret. It is allowed because the solution is not unique: for an orthogonal T, the loadings ΛT imply exactly the correlations Λ does, since T and its inverse cancel. Rotation therefore cannot improve fit or change the communalities. It only chooses which version of one solution you read.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Varimax", d: "The standard orthogonal rotation, which holds the factors uncorrelated and maximizes the variance of the squared loadings so each column ends up with a few large values and many near-zeros. Kaiser normalization, scaling each item's row to unit length first, is on by default in SPSS.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Oblique rotation", d: "A rotation that lets the factors correlate and then reports the correlation. Promax and direct oblimin are the common ones. In psychology it is usually the better choice, because a theory about two related constructs does not claim they are uncorrelated.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Pattern matrix", d: "After an oblique rotation, the table holding each factor's own contribution to an item with the other factors held constant, which makes a pattern coefficient a regression weight rather than a correlation. Interpret this table. Reading the structure matrix instead is a common error with an oblique solution.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Structure matrix", d: "After an oblique rotation, the table of plain correlations between items and factors. When the factors correlate, these correlations are inflated, so an item that belongs to one factor still correlates moderately with the other. The table then suggests cross-loadings that the pattern matrix shows are not there.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Cross-loading", d: "An item that loads above the threshold on two factors AND whose two loadings are within about .20 of each other, so it identifies neither construct. The second condition is the one people forget: loadings of .62 and .35 both clear the threshold, but the item still belongs to one factor.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Simple structure", d: "The target rotation aims at: every item loading heavily on one factor and near zero on the rest, so the solution can be described in words. The varimax criterion puts a number on how close a solution is to it.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Factor score", d: "A person's position on a latent factor, which is predicted rather than computed or estimated, because it is a real but unobserved quantity belonging to that person. The regression predictor minimizes mean squared error and handles correlated factors; the Bartlett predictor is unbiased and keeps each score tied to its own factor alone.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Expected count", d: "The count a contingency-table cell would hold if the two variables were independent: its row total times its column total, divided by the grand total. Older texts call it an expected frequency. It is rarely a whole number, and every χ² condition is written about it rather than about the observed count.", s: "stats-1/chi-square-tests", l: "Chi-Square Tests" },
      { t: "Chi-square component", d: "One cell's contribution to χ², (O − E)² divided by E. The components add up to the statistic, so the largest one marks the cell that departs most from independence. That is the cell to describe in a results paragraph.", s: "stats-1/chi-square-tests", l: "Chi-Square Tests" },
      { t: "Conditional distribution", d: "The distribution of one variable inside a single row or column of a two-way table, such as the recovery split among treated patients only. Independence means every row has the same conditional distribution, so comparing the rows shows what a chi-square test is testing.", s: "stats-1/chi-square-tests", l: "Chi-Square Tests" },
      { t: "F-ratio", d: "The test statistic in ANOVA and regression: the ratio of explained (between) variance to unexplained (within) variance.", s: "stats-2/one-way-anova", l: "One-Way ANOVA" },
      { t: "Factorial ANOVA", d: "An ANOVA with two or more factors, letting you test each main effect and their interaction at once.", s: "stats-2/factorial-anova-two-way", l: "Factorial ANOVA (Two-Way)" },
      { t: "Fixed effect", d: "A model term whose specific levels are the ones you care about and want to estimate directly (contrast with random effect).", s: "stats-3/mixed-and-multilevel-models", l: "Mixed & Multilevel Models" },
      { t: "Generalized linear model (GLM)", d: "A regression framework that extends linear models to other outcomes (counts, binary) via a link function and non-normal errors.", s: "stats-3/generalized-linear-models", l: "Generalized Linear Models" },
      { t: "Heteroscedasticity", d: "Non-constant spread of residuals across the range of a predictor, a violation of a key regression assumption.", s: "stats-2/regression-diagnostics", l: "Regression Diagnostics" },
      { t: "Hit rate & false-alarm rate", d: "The two numbers a yes/no detection task produces: the share of signal trials answered 'yes' (hits) and the share of noise trials answered 'yes' (false alarms). Together they give d′ and c.", s: "stats-3/signal-detection-theory", l: "Signal Detection Theory" },
      { t: "Homogeneity of variance", d: "The assumption that compared groups have equal population variances; checked with tests like Levene's.", s: "stats-2/assumptions-and-when-they-break", l: "ANOVA & t-Test Assumptions" },
      { t: "Independent-samples t-test", d: "A test comparing the means of two separate, unrelated groups.", s: "stats-1/independent-samples-t-test", l: "Independent Samples t-Test" },
      { t: "Indirect effect", d: "In mediation, the part of an X→Y effect that passes through a mediator M (the X→M→Y path).", s: "stats-3/mediation-and-indirect-effects", l: "Mediation & Indirect Effects" },
      { t: "Inferential statistics", d: "Using a sample to make uncertainty-aware statements about the larger population you didn't measure.", s: "stats-1/what-is-statistics", l: "What Is Statistics?" },
      { t: "Interaction effect", d: "When the effect of one variable on the outcome depends on the level of another variable.", s: "stats-2/interactions-in-regression", l: "Interactions in Regression" },
      { t: "Interquartile range (IQR)", d: "The spread of the middle 50% of the data (Q3 − Q1), a measure of spread that ignores extreme outliers.", s: "stats-1/describing-data", l: "Describing Data" },
      { t: "Quartile", d: "A value cutting the sorted data at a quarter (Q1), a half (the median) or three quarters (Q3). Conventions differ. A textbook takes the median of each half, while software interpolates (R and Python share one rule, SPSS uses another), so Q1 and Q3 can legitimately differ between a hand calculation and a printout.", s: "stats-1/describing-data", l: "Describing Data" },
      { t: "Five-number summary", d: "Minimum, Q1, median, Q3 and maximum: the five values a boxplot draws, and the resistant counterpart to reporting a mean with a standard deviation.", s: "stats-1/describing-data", l: "Describing Data" },
      { t: "Intraclass correlation (ICC)", d: "The share of total variance that is due to differences between groups or clusters: how much observations within a group resemble each other.", s: "stats-3/mixed-and-multilevel-models", l: "Mixed & Multilevel Models" },
      { t: "Pseudoreplication", d: "Analyzing clustered observations as if each were an independent unit, counting 400 pupils in 20 classes as 400. It inflates false positives for anything compared between clusters.", s: "stats-3/mixed-and-multilevel-models", l: "Mixed & Multilevel Models" },
      { t: "Design effect (deff)", d: "How much a clustered design inflates variance versus simple random sampling, deff ≈ 1 + (m − 1)ρ. Dividing your sample size by it gives the number of independent observations you effectively have.", s: "methods/sampling-methods", l: "Sampling Methods" },
      { t: "Ecological fallacy", d: "Concluding something about individuals from a relationship measured between group averages. The group-level association can be far larger than the individual one, or point the opposite way.", s: "methods/observational-designs", l: "Observational Designs" },
      { t: "Ecological study", d: "An observational design whose unit is a group (country, county, school, year) with exposure and outcome both measured as group averages.", s: "methods/observational-designs", l: "Observational Designs" },
      { t: "McNemar's test", d: "The chi-square test for paired categorical data: cross each person's before-answer with their own after-answer and test only the two off-diagonal cells, (b − c)² ⁄ (b + c).", s: "stats-1/chi-square-tests", l: "Chi-Square Tests" },
      { t: "Forest plot", d: "The standard meta-analysis picture: one row per study (square sized by weight, whiskers for its CI) and a diamond for the pooled effect.", s: "stats-3/meta-analysis", l: "Meta-Analysis & Forest Plots" },
      { t: "Hazard ratio", d: "How many times higher one group's instantaneous event rate is than another's. It is the effect size of survival analysis.", s: "stats-3/survival-analysis", l: "Survival Analysis" },
      { t: "Heterogeneity (I²)", d: "In meta-analysis, the share of between-study spread reflecting real differences in effects rather than sampling noise.", s: "stats-3/meta-analysis", l: "Meta-Analysis & Forest Plots" },
      { t: "Imputation", d: "Filling in missing values. Mean imputation shrinks variances and correlations. Multiple imputation carries the uncertainty about the missing values into the standard errors.", s: "stats-3/missing-data", l: "Missing Data & Imputation" },
      { t: "Just-noticeable difference (JND)", d: "The precision of a psychometric fit: half the stimulus distance between the curve's 25% and 75% points. A steep curve gives a small JND, which means sharp discrimination.", s: "stats-3/psychometric-functions", l: "Psychometric Functions & the PSE" },
      { t: "Kaplan–Meier estimator", d: "A step-curve estimate of survival probability over time, using censored observations for exactly as long as they were observed.", s: "stats-3/survival-analysis", l: "Survival Analysis" },
      { t: "Log-rank test", d: "A test comparing whole survival curves by accumulating observed-minus-expected events at every event time.", s: "stats-3/survival-analysis", l: "Survival Analysis" },
      { t: "Least squares", d: "The fitting principle behind linear regression: choose the line that minimizes the sum of squared residuals.", s: "stats-1/simple-linear-regression", l: "Simple Linear Regression" },
      { t: "Residual plot", d: "A scatterplot of the residuals against x (or against the fitted values), with a line at zero. With the fitted line subtracted, patterns are easy to see: a shapeless band means a straight line fits, an arch means the relationship bends, and a wedge means the scatter grows across the range.", s: "stats-1/simple-linear-regression", l: "Simple Linear Regression" },
      { t: "Extrapolation", d: "Reading a fitted line outside the range of x the data covered. The line still gives a prediction there, with no sign that it is outside the data, so you have to check the range yourself.", s: "stats-1/simple-linear-regression", l: "Simple Linear Regression" },
      { t: "Lurking variable", d: "A variable that was not measured and is not in the analysis, yet influences both of the variables that were. It can weaken a real relationship, create one that is not there, or reverse the direction of a fitted line when the groups it defines are pooled.", s: "stats-1/simple-linear-regression", l: "Simple Linear Regression" },
      { t: "Leverage (hat value h)", d: "How much potential a case has to move a regression line, decided purely by how unusual its predictor values are. Leverages always sum to p (the number of estimated parameters), so average leverage is p/n and the usual flags are 2p/n and 3p/n. A high-leverage case distorts the fit only when it also has a large residual.", s: "stats-2/assumptions-of-regression", l: "Assumptions of Regression" },
      { t: "Likelihood", d: "How probable the observed data are under a given parameter value. It is the basis of both maximum-likelihood and Bayesian estimation.", s: "stats-3/bayesian-thinking", l: "Bayesian Thinking" },
      { t: "Likelihood function", d: "The same probability formula read the other way round: the data are held fixed and the parameter varies. P(9 of 12 | p) as a function of p is a likelihood function. It does not sum or integrate to 1 over the parameter, so it is not a probability distribution and cannot become one without a prior.", s: "stats-3/bayesian-thinking", l: "Bayesian Thinking" },
      { t: "Grid approximation", d: "Bayes' rule run as a table: a short list of candidate parameter values, a prior column, a likelihood column, their product, and a posterior obtained by dividing each product by the column total. It works when the posterior has no closed form, and it is the version an exam asks you to do by hand.", s: "stats-3/bayesian-thinking", l: "Bayesian Thinking" },
      { t: "Marginal likelihood", d: "The total of the prior-times-likelihood column, and the denominator in Bayes' rule. Also called the evidence. It is the probability of the observed data averaged over every hypothesis with its prior weight. Dividing by it scales the posterior to sum to 1.", s: "stats-3/bayesian-thinking", l: "Bayesian Thinking" },
      { t: "Conjugate prior", d: "A prior family whose posterior is in the same family, so the posterior can be written down in closed form. Beta is conjugate to the binomial likelihood: a Beta(a, b) prior plus k successes in n trials gives Beta(a + k, b + n - k). Before MCMC most practical Bayesian work used conjugate priors, and they are still used to check a sampler against an exact answer.", s: "stats-3/bayesian-thinking", l: "Bayesian Thinking" },
      { t: "Non-informative prior", d: "A prior that spreads its weight evenly, so the posterior is driven almost entirely by the data. It is easy to defend and usually the default in an assignment, but it is still a choice. A flat prior over a list of candidates says they were all equally plausible and rules out everything not on the list.", s: "stats-3/bayesian-thinking", l: "Bayesian Thinking" },
      { t: "Link function", d: "The function in a generalized linear model that connects the mean of the outcome to a straight line: g(μ) = b₀ + b₁x. Identity for ordinary regression, logit for probabilities, log for counts. The coefficients are linear on the link scale, so to interpret them you transform back with the inverse link.", s: "stats-3/generalized-linear-models", l: "Generalized Linear Models" },
      { t: "Logistic regression", d: "A model for a binary outcome that predicts the log-odds of 'yes' with an S-shaped curve bounded between 0 and 1.", s: "stats-2/logistic-regression", l: "Logistic Regression" },
      { t: "Main effect", d: "The overall effect of one factor on the outcome, averaged across the levels of the other factors.", s: "stats-2/factorial-anova-two-way", l: "Factorial ANOVA (Two-Way)" },
      { t: "Marginal mean", d: "The average of the cell means in one row or column of a factorial design, written in the margin of the table. A main effect is the difference between two marginal means. Because it averages the cells rather than the people, it matches the plain group average only when every cell holds the same number of observations.", s: "stats-2/factorial-anova-two-way", l: "Factorial ANOVA (Two-Way)" },
      { t: "Simple main effect", d: "The effect of one factor at a single level of the other, computed inside one row or column rather than across the whole design. Each main effect is the average of its own simple main effects, and an interaction test compares two simple main effects.", s: "stats-2/factorial-anova-two-way", l: "Factorial ANOVA (Two-Way)" },
      { t: "Moderator", d: "A variable that changes the strength or the direction of another variable's effect. Moderation is the same claim an interaction makes, in the vocabulary of modeling rather than of design.", s: "stats-2/factorial-anova-two-way", l: "Factorial ANOVA (Two-Way)" },
      { t: "Cell (factorial design)", d: "One combination of levels, one from each factor: a 2 × 3 design has six cells. A design is fully crossed when every cell is filled and balanced when every cell holds the same number of observations.", s: "stats-2/factorial-anova-two-way", l: "Factorial ANOVA (Two-Way)" },
      { t: "Mean", d: "The arithmetic average — every value summed and divided by the count. Sensitive to outliers.", s: "stats-1/describing-data", l: "Describing Data" },
      { t: "Median", d: "The middle value when the data are ordered, a measure of center robust to extreme values.", s: "stats-1/describing-data", l: "Describing Data" },
      { t: "Mediation", d: "A causal chain in which a predictor affects an outcome partly or wholly through an intervening variable.", s: "stats-3/mediation-and-indirect-effects", l: "Mediation & Indirect Effects" },
      { t: "MCAR / MAR / MNAR", d: "The three missingness mechanisms: pure chance, driven by other observed variables, or driven by the missing values themselves.", s: "stats-3/missing-data", l: "Missing Data & Imputation" },
      { t: "Meta-analysis", d: "Pooling effect sizes across studies, each weighted by its precision, into one combined estimate with a confidence interval.", s: "stats-3/meta-analysis", l: "Meta-Analysis & Forest Plots" },
      { t: "Training vs. test data", d: "Data used to fit a model versus data reserved to evaluate it. Only test performance can reveal overfitting.", s: "stats-3/cross-validation-and-overfitting", l: "Cross-Validation & Overfitting" },
      { t: "Moderation", d: "When a third variable changes the strength or direction of the relationship between two others (an interaction).", s: "stats-2/interactions-in-regression", l: "Interactions in Regression" },
      { t: "Multicollinearity", d: "High correlation among predictors in a regression, which inflates standard errors and makes coefficients unstable.", s: "stats-2/multicollinearity-and-variable-selection", l: "Multicollinearity & Variable Selection" },
      { t: "General-to-specific", d: "A model-selection procedure: start from a model holding every candidate predictor, drop the weakest, refit, and repeat. It has the same problem as stepwise selection: the surviving p-values were chosen for being small.", s: "stats-2/multicollinearity-and-variable-selection", l: "Multicollinearity & Variable Selection" },
      { t: "MANOVA", d: "Multivariate ANOVA. Tests whether groups differ on a set of outcome variables at once, taking the correlations among the outcomes into account.", s: "stats-3/manova", l: "MANOVA & Multivariate Tests" },
      { t: "MANCOVA", d: "MANOVA with covariates: compares groups on several outcomes simultaneously while adjusting for baseline variables.", s: "stats-3/manova", l: "MANOVA & Multivariate Tests" },
      { t: "Mahalanobis distance", d: "Distance measured relative to the data's covariance structure: 'how many standard deviations away, accounting for correlations.' The metric behind multivariate tests.", s: "stats-3/manova", l: "MANOVA & Multivariate Tests" },
      { t: "Multilevel / mixed model", d: "A model for nested data (students in schools, trials in people) with both fixed effects and random effects.", s: "stats-3/mixed-and-multilevel-models", l: "Mixed & Multilevel Models" },
      { t: "Multiple regression", d: "Predicting an outcome from several predictors at once, so each coefficient is the effect holding the others constant.", s: "stats-2/multiple-regression", l: "Multiple Regression" },
      { t: "Omitted-variable bias", d: "Leaving out x₂ makes the coefficient on x₁ estimate β₁ + β₂δ, where δ is the slope of x₂ on x₁. The bias β₂δ is zero unless the missing variable is related to both x₁ and the outcome, and its sign is the product of the two signs.", s: "stats-2/multiple-regression", l: "Multiple Regression" },
      { t: "Multiple correlation (R)", d: "The square root of R², and the plain correlation between the observed outcome and the model's fitted values. It is never negative, however many coefficients inside it are.", s: "stats-2/multiple-regression", l: "Multiple Regression" },
      { t: "Non-parametric test", d: "A test that avoids assuming a specific distribution, often by working with ranks (e.g. Mann-Whitney, Kruskal-Wallis).", s: "stats-2/non-parametric-alternatives", l: "Non-Parametric Alternatives" },
      { t: "Normal distribution", d: "The symmetric bell-shaped distribution defined by its mean and standard deviation; central to much of inference.", s: "stats-1/z-scores-and-the-normal-distribution", l: "Z-Scores & The Normal Distribution" },
      { t: "Null hypothesis (H₀)", d: "The default 'nothing is going on' claim — no effect, no difference — that a test tries to rule out.", s: "stats-1/hypothesis-testing-logic", l: "Hypothesis Testing Logic" },
      { t: "Odds ratio", d: "In logistic regression, the multiplicative change in the odds of the outcome for a one-unit rise in a predictor.", s: "stats-2/logistic-regression", l: "Logistic Regression" },
      { t: "Outlier", d: "An observation far from the rest of the data, which can strongly distort means, variances, and regression lines.", s: "stats-1/describing-data", l: "Describing Data" },
      { t: "Offset (in a GLM)", d: "A predictor forced into a count model with its coefficient fixed at 1, almost always log(exposure): follow-up time, patient-days, population at risk. It converts a model of counts into a model of rates, since log(count) − log(exposure) is log(rate). Leaving it out of unequal-exposure data can reverse the finding outright.", s: "stats-3/generalized-linear-models", l: "Generalized Linear Models" },
      { t: "Overdispersion", d: "Counts that vary more than the fitted Poisson model allows, since the Poisson model assumes variance = mean. Symptoms are a deviance/df ratio well above 1 and standard errors that are too small. Usual fixes are a quasi-Poisson model or a negative binomial one; unequal exposure with no offset is a common hidden cause.", s: "stats-3/generalized-linear-models", l: "Generalized Linear Models" },
      { t: "Overfitting", d: "A model that captures noise as if it were signal — great on its own data, poor on new data.", s: "stats-3/cross-validation-and-overfitting", l: "Cross-Validation & Overfitting" },
      { t: "p-value", d: "The probability of data at least this extreme if the null hypothesis were true, not the probability the null is true.", s: "stats-1/hypothesis-testing-logic", l: "Hypothesis Testing Logic" },
      { t: "Parameter", d: "A true, usually unknown number describing a population (like the mean μ) that we estimate from a sample.", s: "stats-1/what-is-statistics", l: "What Is Statistics?" },
      { t: "Population", d: "The entire set of people or things you want to draw conclusions about.", s: "stats-1/what-is-statistics", l: "What Is Statistics?" },
      { t: "Post-hoc test", d: "A follow-up comparison run after a significant ANOVA to find which specific groups differ, with corrected error rates.", s: "stats-2/post-hoc-tests", l: "Post-Hoc Tests" },
      { t: "Point of subjective equality (PSE)", d: "The stimulus level at which an observer picks each of two responses equally often: the 50% point of the psychometric function, marking the subjective midpoint.", s: "stats-3/psychometric-functions", l: "Psychometric Functions & the PSE" },
      { t: "Posterior distribution", d: "In Bayesian inference, your updated belief about a parameter after combining the prior with the data.", s: "stats-3/bayesian-estimation", l: "Bayesian Estimation" },
      { t: "Pillai's trace", d: "A multivariate test statistic for MANOVA, the most robust of the four to assumption violations, and a sensible default to report.", s: "stats-3/manova", l: "MANOVA & Multivariate Tests" },
      { t: "Power (statistical)", d: "The probability a test detects a real effect of a given size, or 1 minus the Type II error rate.", s: "stats-3/effect-size-and-power", l: "Effect Size & Power" },
      { t: "Principal component analysis (PCA)", d: "A technique that re-expresses correlated variables as a smaller set of uncorrelated components capturing most variance.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Prior distribution", d: "In Bayesian inference, the belief about a parameter you hold before seeing the current data.", s: "stats-3/bayesian-thinking", l: "Bayesian Thinking" },
      { t: "Psychometric function", d: "The S-shaped curve linking a stimulus level to the probability of one response in a two-choice task, fit by maximum likelihood to read off the PSE and JND.", s: "stats-3/psychometric-functions", l: "Psychometric Functions & the PSE" },
      { t: "R² (coefficient of determination)", d: "The proportion of variance in the outcome explained by the model, from 0 (nothing) to 1 (perfect).", s: "stats-1/simple-linear-regression", l: "Simple Linear Regression" },
      { t: "Random effect", d: "A model term whose levels are a random sample from a larger population (e.g. specific participants or schools).", s: "stats-3/mixed-and-multilevel-models", l: "Mixed & Multilevel Models" },
      { t: "Rank-biserial correlation", d: "The effect size that belongs with a Mann–Whitney or Wilcoxon test: the win rate minus the loss rate across every cross-group pairing, or equivalently 2 × P(superiority) − 1. It runs from 0 (the groups interleave evenly) to 1 (every value in one group beats every value in the other). JASP prints it under Effect size; it is not the same number as r = z/√N.", s: "stats-2/non-parametric-alternatives", l: "Non-Parametric Alternatives" },
      { t: "Regression coefficient", d: "The predicted change in the outcome for a one-unit increase in a predictor, holding the others constant.", s: "stats-2/multiple-regression", l: "Multiple Regression" },
      { t: "Repeated-measures ANOVA", d: "An ANOVA for designs where the same subjects are measured under several conditions, accounting for within-subject correlation.", s: "stats-2/repeated-measures-anova", l: "Repeated Measures ANOVA" },
      { t: "Residual", d: "The gap between an observed value and the value a model predicts: what the model failed to explain.", s: "stats-2/regression-diagnostics", l: "Regression Diagnostics" },
      { t: "Sample", d: "The subset of the population you actually measure and compute statistics from.", s: "stats-1/what-is-statistics", l: "What Is Statistics?" },
      { t: "Sampling distribution", d: "The distribution of a statistic (like the mean) across all possible samples of the same size. Inference from a sample rests on it.", s: "stats-1/sampling-distributions", l: "Sampling Distributions" },
      { t: "Skewness", d: "Asymmetry in a distribution: a long right tail is positive skew, a long left tail is negative skew.", s: "stats-1/describing-data", l: "Describing Data" },
      { t: "Correction for attenuation", d: "Dividing an observed correlation by √(rxx · ryy) to estimate the correlation between true scores. It is unbiased on average but noticeably noisier than the raw r, and it can exceed 1.", s: "stats-1/correlation", l: "Correlation" },
      { t: "Standard deviation", d: "The typical distance of values from their mean, or the square root of the variance.", s: "stats-1/describing-data", l: "Describing Data" },
      { t: "Sum of squares", d: "The total of every squared deviation from a mean, written SS. Dividing it by its degrees of freedom gives a variance, so sums of squares appear throughout the t-test, ANOVA and regression.", s: "stats-1/describing-data", l: "Describing Data" },
      { t: "Standard error", d: "The standard deviation of a statistic's sampling distribution: how much an estimate varies from sample to sample.", s: "stats-1/sampling-distributions", l: "Sampling Distributions" },
      { t: "Statistic", d: "A number computed from a sample (like x̄) used to estimate a population parameter.", s: "stats-1/what-is-statistics", l: "What Is Statistics?" },
      { t: "Studentized residual", d: "A regression residual divided by its own standard error, s√(1 − h), rather than by one shared value. This matters because a high-leverage case pulls the line toward itself and so has an artificially small raw residual. SPSS's Casewise diagnostics box flags on the plain standardized residual, so the studentized version is a separate tick under Save…", s: "stats-2/assumptions-of-regression", l: "Assumptions of Regression" },
      { t: "t-test", d: "A test comparing means using the t-distribution, which accounts for extra uncertainty when the SD is estimated from a small sample.", s: "stats-1/one-sample-and-paired-t-tests", l: "One-Sample & Paired t-Tests" },
      { t: "Type I error", d: "A false positive — rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true. Its rate is alpha.", s: "stats-1/hypothesis-testing-logic", l: "Hypothesis Testing Logic" },
      { t: "Type II error", d: "A false negative — failing to reject a null hypothesis that is actually false. Its rate is beta.", s: "stats-3/effect-size-and-power", l: "Effect Size & Power" },
      { t: "Unbiased estimator", d: "An estimator whose sampling distribution is centered on the truth: repeat the study forever and the average estimate equals the population value. Distinct from consistency (the distribution tightening onto that value as n grows) and from efficiency (how small its standard error is).", s: "stats-1/sampling-distributions", l: "Sampling Distributions" },
      { t: "Variance", d: "The average squared distance of values from their mean, a foundational measure of spread.", s: "stats-1/describing-data", l: "Describing Data" },
      { t: "Variance inflation factor (VIF)", d: "A diagnostic for multicollinearity: how much a predictor's variance is inflated by its correlation with other predictors.", s: "stats-2/multicollinearity-and-variable-selection", l: "Multicollinearity & Variable Selection" },
      { t: "Wilks' lambda (Λ)", d: "A MANOVA test statistic: the proportion of outcome variance NOT explained by group membership, so smaller means a stronger multivariate effect.", s: "stats-3/manova", l: "MANOVA & Multivariate Tests" },
      { t: "Z-score", d: "How many standard deviations a value is above or below the mean: z = (x − μ) / σ.", s: "stats-1/z-scores-and-the-normal-distribution", l: "Z-Scores & The Normal Distribution" },
      { t: "Critical value", d: "The cutoff a test statistic must beat for significance at your chosen alpha: the table value (e.g. z = 1.96 for two-tailed α = .05).", s: "stats-1/hypothesis-testing-logic", l: "Hypothesis Testing Logic" },
      { t: "Rejection region", d: "The tail area(s) of the test statistic's distribution beyond the critical value. A statistic in that region means p < α, so you reject the null.", s: "stats-1/hypothesis-testing-logic", l: "Hypothesis Testing Logic" },
      { t: "One-tailed vs. two-tailed test", d: "Whether your alternative hypothesis predicts a direction (one tail) or just 'a difference' (both tails). Two-tailed is the cautious default.", s: "stats-1/hypothesis-testing-logic", l: "Hypothesis Testing Logic" },
      { t: "z-interval", d: "The confidence interval for a mean when the population SD is known: x̄ ± z*·σ/√n, with z* of 1.645, 1.960 or 2.576 for 90, 95 or 99% confidence. Textbooks teach it first because the arithmetic is short. With real data σ is unknown, so you replace σ with s and z* with t*.", s: "stats-1/confidence-intervals", l: "Confidence Intervals" },
      { t: "Margin of error", d: "The half-width of a confidence interval, m = z*·σ/√n for a mean, and what a poll means by '±3 points'. Only three things change it: the confidence level, the spread of the population, and the sample size. Because n enters through √n, halving the margin needs four times the data.", s: "stats-1/confidence-intervals", l: "Confidence Intervals" },
      { t: "Practical significance", d: "Whether an effect is big enough to matter, which is a different question from statistical significance (whether it is distinguishable from zero). Given enough data, a difference far too small to act on will be significant at any alpha you pick. So report an effect size and a confidence interval with every p-value.", s: "stats-1/hypothesis-testing-logic", l: "Hypothesis Testing Logic" },
      { t: "Welch's t-test", d: "The unequal-variances version of the independent t-test, with no pooling and adjusted df. It is robust enough that many statistics programs use it by default.", s: "stats-1/independent-samples-t-test", l: "Independent Samples t-Test" },
      { t: "Standard error of the mean (SE)", d: "s/√n: the estimated spread of the sample mean's own sampling distribution, and the denominator of every t statistic. It shrinks with the square root of n, so quadrupling the sample halves it.", s: "stats-1/one-sample-and-paired-t-tests", l: "One-Sample & Paired t-Tests" },
      { t: "Conservative degrees of freedom", d: "The hand rule for a two-sample t: df = min(n₁ − 1, n₂ − 1). That is fewer df than the software uses, so the critical value is larger, the interval wider and the p-value bigger. It can turn a significant result into a nonsignificant one, but never the reverse.", s: "stats-1/independent-samples-t-test", l: "Independent Samples t-Test" },
      { t: "Pooled t-test", d: "The equal-variances two-sample t-test, which combines both samples into one estimate of a shared variance and uses df = n₁ + n₂ − 2. Usually called Student's t-test when it is being set against Welch's, and valid only when the two population variances are equal.", s: "stats-1/independent-samples-t-test", l: "Independent Samples t-Test" },
      { t: "Mann-Whitney U test", d: "The rank-based alternative to the independent-samples t-test for two unrelated groups.", s: "stats-2/non-parametric-alternatives", l: "Non-Parametric Alternatives" },
      { t: "Wilcoxon signed-rank test", d: "The rank-based alternative for one-sample or paired designs, built on the signed ranks of the difference scores.", s: "stats-2/non-parametric-alternatives", l: "Non-Parametric Alternatives" },
      { t: "Kruskal-Wallis test", d: "The rank-based counterpart of one-way ANOVA for three or more independent groups.", s: "stats-2/non-parametric-alternatives", l: "Non-Parametric Alternatives" },
      { t: "Friedman test", d: "The rank-based counterpart of repeated-measures ANOVA for three or more within-person conditions.", s: "stats-2/non-parametric-alternatives", l: "Non-Parametric Alternatives" },
      { t: "Rank sum", d: "The total of the ranks belonging to one group once every value has been pooled and ranked. All the rank sums together must come to N(N + 1)/2, which is a useful arithmetic check. Mann-Whitney's U, Kruskal-Wallis's H and Friedman's statistic are all computed from rank sums alone.", s: "stats-2/non-parametric-alternatives", l: "Non-Parametric Alternatives" },
      { t: "U statistic", d: "The Mann-Whitney test statistic: the number of times a value from one group is beaten by a value from the other. It is computed as U₁ = n₁n₂ + n₁(n₁ + 1)/2 − R₁, and U₁ and its partner U₂ sum to n₁n₂. The test uses the smaller of the two and is significant when it is at or below the table's critical value.", s: "stats-2/non-parametric-alternatives", l: "Non-Parametric Alternatives" },
      { t: "Signed rank", d: "A difference score's rank by absolute size, with the original sign reattached. The Wilcoxon signed-ranks test drops every pair whose difference is zero, adds the positive ranks into T₊ and the negative ones into T₋, and takes the smaller of the two as W.", s: "stats-2/non-parametric-alternatives", l: "Non-Parametric Alternatives" },
      { t: "Critical-value table", d: "A printed table of the value a statistic has to reach (or, for U and W, fall to) before the result counts as significant at a stated α. A rank statistic's distribution under the null hypothesis depends only on the sample sizes, so it can be counted once and reused.", s: "stats-2/non-parametric-alternatives", l: "Non-Parametric Alternatives" },
      { t: "Tukey's HSD", d: "A post-hoc procedure built for all pairwise comparisons after ANOVA, controlling the familywise error rate with less lost power than Bonferroni.", s: "stats-2/post-hoc-tests", l: "Post-Hoc Tests" },
      { t: "Cramér's V", d: "An effect size for chi-square tests of association, scaled 0–1, so a significant result can also be judged for strength.", s: "stats-1/chi-square-tests", l: "Chi-Square Tests" },
      { t: "Communality (h²)", d: "The share of an item's variance that the retained factors explain, the sum of its squared loadings. Its complement is the item's uniqueness: specific content plus measurement error.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Measurement model", d: "The equation factor analysis is built on: an answer is the item's own mean plus its loading times the person's factor score plus that item's error, y = μ + βξ + ε. The factor is standardized to mean 0 and variance 1 because a latent variable has no units of its own, so something has to fix its scale.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Factor loading (β)", d: "How strongly one item responds to one factor. With the factor and the items standardized it is the correlation between them, and two items' model-implied correlation is the product of their loadings, σ(ij) = β(i)β(j).", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Uniqueness (ψ)", d: "The part of a standardized item's variance the factors do not explain, 1 − h². It is partly content specific to that item and partly measurement error. It is the second half of the split σ(ii) = β(i)² + ψ(ii), and it appears on the diagonal of a residual matrix.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Model-implied correlation", d: "What a set of loadings predicts the correlation between two items should be, before looking at the data again. Subtracting it from the observed correlation gives the residual, and extraction is the search for loadings that make every residual as small as it can be.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Principal axis factoring (PAF)", d: "An extraction method that puts estimated communalities on the diagonal of the correlation matrix in place of the 1s, solves, re-estimates the communalities from the new loadings and repeats until they settle. It makes no assumption about the distribution of the data, so it suits Likert items.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Maximum likelihood extraction", d: "An extraction method that finds the loadings making the observed data most likely under multivariate normality. The normality assumption is a real limitation, but it gives a likelihood, and with it a chi-square goodness-of-fit test of whether the number of factors you asked for is enough.", s: "stats-3/factor-analysis-pca", l: "Factor Analysis / PCA" },
      { t: "Spearman rank correlation", d: "Pearson's r computed on ranks. It captures any consistently rising or falling (monotone) relationship and resists outliers.", s: "stats-1/correlation", l: "Correlation" },
      { t: "Spearman's rho (ρₛ)", d: "The symbol for the Spearman rank correlation, and the name of its hand shortcut: ρₛ = 1 − 6Σd²/(n(n² − 1)), where d is each pair's difference in ranks. The shortcut is algebraically exact only when nothing ties; with ties, run Pearson on the two rank columns instead.", s: "stats-2/non-parametric-alternatives", l: "Non-Parametric Alternatives" },
      { t: "Odds", d: "The probability of an event divided by the probability of its absence: p / (1 − p). Logistic regression models it on the log scale, as log-odds.", s: "stats-2/logistic-regression", l: "Logistic Regression" },
      { t: "Sphericity", d: "The repeated-measures ANOVA assumption that all pairwise condition-difference scores have equal variance; violations get a Greenhouse–Geisser df correction.", s: "stats-2/repeated-measures-anova", l: "Repeated Measures ANOVA" },
      { t: "Within-subjects factor", d: "A factor whose levels every participant experiences, so its effect is judged against the person-by-condition residual rather than against how much people differ. Its error has (n − 1)(k − 1) degrees of freedom.", s: "stats-2/repeated-measures-anova", l: "Repeated Measures ANOVA" },
      { t: "Between-subjects factor", d: "A factor whose levels different participants experience, so its effect is judged against variation between people. In a mixed design it is tested in its own table, on N − g degrees of freedom, where N counts participants rather than observations.", s: "stats-2/repeated-measures-anova", l: "Repeated Measures ANOVA" },
      { t: "Mixed design", d: "A study with at least one between-subjects factor and at least one within-subjects factor, such as two treatment groups measured at three timepoints. The analysis prints two tables because the two kinds of factor are tested against different error terms. The interaction is usually the effect the study was designed to find.", s: "stats-2/repeated-measures-anova", l: "Repeated Measures ANOVA" },
      { t: "Order effect", d: "Any change in a response that comes from a condition's position in the sequence rather than from the condition itself. Practice makes later conditions look better and fatigue makes them look worse. Counterbalancing is used to cancel these effects out.", s: "stats-2/repeated-measures-anova", l: "Repeated Measures ANOVA" },
      { t: "Simpson's paradox", d: "A trend that appears in aggregated data but reverses (or vanishes) within every subgroup, caused by an ignored lurking variable.", s: "stats-2/multiple-regression", l: "Multiple Regression" },
      { t: "Poisson distribution", d: "The classic distribution for counts of events in a fixed window, with variance equal to its mean, the starting point for count GLMs.", s: "stats-3/generalized-linear-models", l: "Generalized Linear Models" },
      { t: "Construct", d: "An abstract idea a study aims to measure — stress, intelligence, aggression — that exists in theory and must be pinned to a concrete measure before it can be studied.", s: "methods/variables-and-operationalization", l: "Variables & Operationalization" },
      { t: "Operationalization", d: "Translating a construct into a specific, measurable procedure: defining 'stress' as salivary cortisol in µg/dL, say. One construct can be operationalized many ways.", s: "methods/variables-and-operationalization", l: "Variables & Operationalization" },
      { t: "Independent variable (IV)", d: "The presumed cause in a study, the variable you manipulate or compare across groups (e.g. drug vs. placebo).", s: "methods/variables-and-operationalization", l: "Variables & Operationalization" },
      { t: "Dependent variable (DV)", d: "The outcome you measure to see whether the independent variable had an effect.", s: "methods/variables-and-operationalization", l: "Variables & Operationalization" },
      { t: "Falsifiability", d: "The requirement that a scientific hypothesis make a risky prediction some possible result could refute. An unfalsifiable claim is consistent with every outcome, so no result can count against it.", s: "methods/from-question-to-hypothesis", l: "From Question to Hypothesis" },
      { t: "Reliability", d: "The consistency of a measure: does it give the same answer under the same conditions? Types include test–retest, inter-rater, and internal consistency. For a single indicator in a factor model it is the share of that item's variance the factor accounts for, which on standardized items is exactly its communality.", s: "methods/reliability-and-validity", l: "Reliability & Validity" },
      { t: "Validity", d: "Whether a measure actually captures the construct it's meant to. Reliability is necessary for validity but never guarantees it.", s: "methods/reliability-and-validity", l: "Reliability & Validity" },
      { t: "True score", d: "In classical test theory, the value a person would get by averaging endless independent measurements, the part of an observed score that is not measurement error.", s: "methods/reliability-and-validity", l: "Reliability & Validity" },
      { t: "Standard error of measurement (SEM)", d: "SD × √(1 − reliability): the spread of measurement error around one person's score. It converts a reliability coefficient into an error band you can report around a score.", s: "methods/reliability-and-validity", l: "Reliability & Validity" },
      { t: "Spearman–Brown prophecy formula", d: "Predicts what a scale's reliability becomes when it is made k times longer with items of the same quality. Each further .10 of α needs more added items than the one before.", s: "methods/reliability-and-validity", l: "Reliability & Validity" },
      { t: "Ceiling effect", d: "Most respondents choosing the top option, so the scale stops separating people who differ. A floor effect is the same failure at the bottom; both shrink correlations and group differences.", s: "methods/survey-and-questionnaire-design", l: "Designing Surveys & Questionnaires" },
      { t: "Cronbach's alpha (α)", d: "A 0–1 index of a scale's internal consistency, how well its items hang together, computed as (k/(k − 1))(1 − Σσ(ii)/σ²(total)). α ≥ .70 is the common minimum. Two cautions apply. α rises with the number of items whatever their quality, so 30 items correlating .20 with each other still give .88. And it measures consistency, not unidimensionality, so a two-factor scale can still have a comfortable α.", s: "methods/reliability-and-validity", l: "Reliability & Validity" },
      { t: "Random assignment", d: "Allocating participants to conditions by chance (e.g. a coin flip). It balances known and unknown confounds in expectation, so an experiment can attribute a difference between conditions to the treatment.", s: "methods/experimental-design-and-randomization", l: "Experiments & Random Assignment" },
      { t: "Random sampling", d: "Drawing participants from the population at random so the sample represents it. Protects external validity (generalization), distinct from random assignment, which protects internal validity.", s: "methods/experimental-design-and-randomization", l: "Experiments & Random Assignment" },
      { t: "Internal validity", d: "Confidence that the treatment, not a confound, caused the observed difference. Random assignment is its strongest guarantee.", s: "methods/experimental-design-and-randomization", l: "Experiments & Random Assignment" },
      { t: "External validity", d: "How far a study's findings generalize beyond its particular sample, setting, and materials. Boosted by representative (random) sampling.", s: "methods/experimental-design-and-randomization", l: "Experiments & Random Assignment" },
      { t: "Placebo", d: "An inert comparison treatment given to a control group so the effect of merely believing one is being treated can be separated from the treatment's real effect.", s: "methods/experimental-design-and-randomization", l: "Experiments & Random Assignment" },
      { t: "Between-subjects design", d: "A design in which each participant experiences only one condition, so conditions are compared across different groups of people.", s: "methods/between-vs-within-designs", l: "Between vs Within Designs" },
      { t: "Within-subjects design", d: "A repeated-measures design in which each participant experiences every condition, serving as their own control. It needs fewer participants, especially when conditions correlate strongly, but is prone to order and carryover effects.", s: "methods/between-vs-within-designs", l: "Between vs Within Designs" },
      { t: "Counterbalancing", d: "Varying the order of conditions across participants (e.g. via a Latin square) so that order and practice effects cancel out on average and are not mistaken for a treatment effect.", s: "methods/between-vs-within-designs", l: "Between vs Within Designs" },
      { t: "Carryover effect", d: "When one condition influences responses in a later condition — a drug still active, a strategy just learned — contaminating within-subjects comparisons.", s: "methods/between-vs-within-designs", l: "Between vs Within Designs" },
      { t: "Quasi-experiment", d: "A study with a treatment, comparison, and outcome but no random assignment. The groups form by choice or circumstance, so hidden differences between them remain a rival explanation.", s: "methods/quasi-experiments", l: "Quasi-Experiments & Natural Experiments" },
      { t: "Difference-in-differences", d: "An estimator that subtracts a comparison group's before-and-after change from the treated group's, removing the gap they started with and any change common to both groups. In a regression it is the coefficient on a treated-by-post interaction, and it is valid only if the trends would have stayed parallel.", s: "stats-3/difference-in-differences", l: "Difference-in-Differences" },
      { t: "Natural experiment", d: "A quasi-experiment in which an outside event (a lottery, law, or arbitrary cutoff) assigns the treatment as-good-as-randomly, so researchers can study a treatment they did not assign themselves.", s: "methods/quasi-experiments", l: "Quasi-Experiments & Natural Experiments" },
      { t: "Regression discontinuity", d: "A design that estimates a causal effect from a strict cutoff on a continuous score. Fit the outcome against the score on each side of the threshold, and the vertical gap between the two fits at the cutoff is the estimate. It is credible because it is local, and it applies only to units near the cutoff.", s: "stats-3/regression-discontinuity", l: "Regression Discontinuity Design" },
      { t: "Cohort study", d: "An observational design that enrolls exposed and unexposed people without the outcome and follows them forward, measuring incidence, so it yields a risk ratio directly and fixes the time-order. It is slow and poorly suited to rare outcomes.", s: "methods/observational-designs", l: "Observational Designs" },
      { t: "Case-control study", d: "An observational design that starts from people who already have the outcome (cases) plus controls, then looks backward at exposure. Efficient for rare outcomes, but because the case:control ratio is chosen, only an odds ratio is estimable.", s: "methods/observational-designs", l: "Observational Designs" },
      { t: "Cross-sectional study", d: "A one-snapshot design measuring exposure and outcome at the same moment. Good for measuring prevalence, but it cannot establish which came first, so it can't show cause and effect.", s: "methods/observational-designs", l: "Observational Designs" },
      { t: "Survivorship bias", d: "The error of studying only the units that remained in the data (survivors) and missing those that dropped out for reasons tied to the outcome. The sample looks complete but leaves out the informative cases.", s: "methods/observational-designs", l: "Observational Designs" },
      { t: "Simple random sampling", d: "A probability method in which every member of the population has an equal chance of selection. It is the unbiased benchmark, but it needs a complete list and can, by luck, miss small subgroups.", s: "methods/sampling-methods", l: "Sampling Methods" },
      { t: "Stratified sampling", d: "Dividing the population into strata (e.g. age bands, regions) and sampling within each, usually proportionally. Guarantees coverage and gives a more precise estimate than simple random sampling for the same n.", s: "methods/sampling-methods", l: "Sampling Methods" },
      { t: "Cluster sampling", d: "Dividing the population into many natural clusters (schools, blocks), randomly selecting a few whole clusters, and measuring everyone in them. Cheap over spread-out populations but far noisier, since cluster members resemble one another.", s: "methods/sampling-methods", l: "Sampling Methods" },
      { t: "Convenience sampling", d: "A non-probability method that recruits whoever is easiest to reach. Its bias does not shrink as the sample grows, so a larger sample only narrows the interval around a biased estimate.", s: "methods/sampling-methods", l: "Sampling Methods" },
      { t: "Sampling frame", d: "The actual list of units you draw a sample from. When it omits part of the target population (coverage error), even a perfectly random draw can't represent whoever the frame left out.", s: "methods/sampling-methods", l: "Sampling Methods" },
      { t: "Nonresponse bias", d: "The distortion that arises when people who don't answer a survey differ systematically from those who do, so the realized sample differs from the intended one.", s: "methods/sampling-methods", l: "Sampling Methods" },
      { t: "Likert scale", d: "An ordered rating scale (e.g. Strongly disagree → Strongly agree). Best practice is 5–7 balanced, word-labeled points. Whether to offer a neutral midpoint (an odd number of points) is a separate design decision.", s: "methods/survey-and-questionnaire-design", l: "Designing Surveys & Questionnaires" },
      { t: "Double-barreled question", d: "A survey item that asks about two things at once ('the pay and the hours'), so respondents who feel differently about the halves cannot give one accurate answer. The fix is to split it into separate items.", s: "methods/survey-and-questionnaire-design", l: "Designing Surveys & Questionnaires" },
      { t: "Leading question", d: "An item worded to signal the expected answer ('Don't you agree that…?'), biasing responses toward it. The remedy is neutral phrasing.", s: "methods/survey-and-questionnaire-design", l: "Designing Surveys & Questionnaires" },
      { t: "Social desirability bias", d: "The tendency to answer sensitive questions to look good rather than truthfully. Reduced by anonymity that is promised and kept, non-identifying items, and softer framing.", s: "methods/survey-and-questionnaire-design", l: "Designing Surveys & Questionnaires" },
      { t: "Acquiescence bias", d: "The tendency of some respondents to agree with statements regardless of content. Reverse-coded items expose it, since straight-line agreeing then contradicts itself.", s: "methods/survey-and-questionnaire-design", l: "Designing Surveys & Questionnaires" },
      { t: "Blinding (masking)", d: "Hiding condition assignments to prevent bias. Single-blind keeps participants unaware (stopping demand characteristics and the placebo effect); double-blind keeps the interacting researchers unaware too (also stopping experimenter-expectancy bias).", s: "methods/bias-and-blinding", l: "Bias, Blinding & Demand Characteristics" },
      { t: "Demand characteristics", d: "Cues that let participants guess a study's purpose, so they respond to their guess about what's wanted rather than behaving naturally. That can create an effect or hide one.", s: "methods/bias-and-blinding", l: "Bias, Blinding & Demand Characteristics" },
      { t: "Experimenter-expectancy effect", d: "When a researcher's hopes unconsciously influence how participants behave or how outcomes are measured (the 'Clever Hans' mechanism). Double-blinding is the standard defense.", s: "methods/bias-and-blinding", l: "Bias, Blinding & Demand Characteristics" },
      { t: "Placebo effect", d: "A real change in outcome produced by the belief that one is being treated, independent of any active ingredient. The nocebo effect is the reverse: harm produced by the belief. A placebo control and blinding isolate it.", s: "methods/bias-and-blinding", l: "Bias, Blinding & Demand Characteristics" },
      { t: "Selection bias", d: "Systematic error arising when the people who end up in a study differ from the population of interest, as when you recruit exercisers at a gym. Unlike random noise, it does not shrink with sample size.", s: "methods/bias-and-blinding", l: "Bias, Blinding & Demand Characteristics" },
      { t: "Replication crisis", d: "The finding that a large share of published results fail to reproduce: in a 2015 project, fewer than 40% of 100 psychology studies replicated. The main causes are p-hacking, forking paths, publication bias, and low power rather than fraud.", s: "methods/the-replication-crisis", l: "The Replication Crisis" },
      { t: "p-hacking", d: "Trying analyses (dropping outliers, adding covariates, testing subgroups, adding participants) until one crosses p < .05. It is usually motivated flexibility rather than fraud, but it still invalidates the p-value.", s: "methods/the-replication-crisis", l: "The Replication Crisis" },
      { t: "Garden of forking paths", d: "The idea that a dataset allows many defensible analyses. If the one you run depends on the data, your p-values are invalid even if you ran only one test, because different data would have led you to different, equally reasonable choices.", s: "methods/the-replication-crisis", l: "The Replication Crisis" },
      { t: "Publication bias", d: "The tendency for significant, novel results to be published while null findings stay unpublished (the 'file drawer'), so the literature over-represents flukes and overstates effects. A funnel plot is designed to reveal this asymmetry.", s: "methods/the-replication-crisis", l: "The Replication Crisis" },
      { t: "HARKing", d: "Hypothesizing After the Results are Known — presenting a pattern found by exploration as though it had been predicted in advance, so a chance finding looks like a confirmed prediction.", s: "methods/the-replication-crisis", l: "The Replication Crisis" },
      { t: "Researcher degrees of freedom", d: "The many small, individually defensible analytic choices (exclusions, transformations, which covariates or measures, when to stop collecting) that together inflate false positives. Taken together, they make up the garden of forking paths.", s: "methods/the-replication-crisis", l: "The Replication Crisis" },
      { t: "Preregistration", d: "Depositing a timestamped hypothesis and analysis plan before collecting or looking at the data, so the analysis can't be steered by the result. Only an analysis fixed this way counts as a confirmatory test.", s: "methods/preregistration-and-open-science", l: "Preregistration & Open Science" },
      { t: "Registered report", d: "A publishing format in which a journal peer-reviews a study's plan before the data exist and grants in-principle acceptance regardless of the eventual results, so publication cannot depend on whether the results are significant.", s: "methods/preregistration-and-open-science", l: "Preregistration & Open Science" },
      { t: "Confirmatory vs exploratory analysis", d: "Confirmatory analysis tests a prediction fixed in advance and yields a valid p-value; exploratory analysis searches the data for patterns and generates hypotheses. Both are legitimate. The problem is presenting exploratory results as confirmatory.", s: "methods/preregistration-and-open-science", l: "Preregistration & Open Science" },
      { t: "Tidy data", d: "A standard table shape where each variable is a column, each observation a row, and each cell one value. Because every tidy table has the same shape, tools can filter, group, plot, and model the data without reshaping it first.", s: "data/tidy-data", l: "Tidy Data" },
      { t: "Wide vs long format", d: "Two layouts for the same data: wide spreads one variable across several columns (Quiz 1, Quiz 2, Final); long stacks them into a key column and a value column. Tidy data is long; some tools (e.g. repeated-measures ANOVA in SPSS) require wide.", s: "data/tidy-data", l: "Tidy Data" },
      { t: "Codebook", d: "A variable-by-variable dictionary recording each column's name, label, type, units or allowed values, and missing codes. It keeps a dataset understandable to you later and to others, and your validation rules are built from it.", s: "data/codebooks-and-documentation", l: "Codebooks & Documentation" },
      { t: "Missing data code", d: "How 'no answer' is stored. A blank cell or NA is safest. Numeric codes like -99 or 999 are risky because, if they are not declared as missing, the software averages them in as real values. Never use 0, which is a legitimate value for many variables.", s: "data/codebooks-and-documentation", l: "Codebooks & Documentation" },
      { t: "Data validation", d: "Catching errors at entry by declaring what a legal value looks like: range checks (0 ≤ age ≤ 120), allowed-value sets (sex ∈ {M, F, Other}), and cross-field logic (birth year = year − age). Unlike proofreading, the rules check every entry the same way.", s: "data/data-entry-and-validation", l: "Data Entry & Validation" },
      { t: "Range check", d: "A validation rule that a numeric field must fall within plausible bounds. It flags impossible values (an age of 511, a 4 ms reaction time) that would otherwise distort means, inflate SDs, and look like influential outliers.", s: "data/data-entry-and-validation", l: "Data Entry & Validation" },
      { t: "Double data entry", d: "Entering the same data twice and comparing the versions cell by cell; disagreements pinpoint typos to check against the source. Standard in clinical trials, it sharply cuts the undetected-error rate.", s: "data/data-entry-and-validation", l: "Data Entry & Validation" },
      { t: "Data-cleaning pipeline", d: "A loop run the same way every time: inspect, document the issues, fix via script, re-inspect, log the decision. Keeping the raw file read-only and every fix in code makes cleaning reproducible, reversible, and auditable.", s: "data/data-cleaning-workflow", l: "The Cleaning Workflow" },
      { t: "Fuzzy duplicate", d: "Two rows that mean the same record but aren't byte-identical: 'Ann Lee' vs 'Ann Lee ' with a trailing space, or a near-repeat. Unlike exact duplicates they need investigation before removal, and long-format data legitimately repeats IDs across rows.", s: "data/data-cleaning-workflow", l: "The Cleaning Workflow" },
      { t: "Sensitivity analysis", d: "Rerunning an analysis under a different reasonable choice, most often with and without a suspicious outlier, and reporting both. It shows the reader how much a conclusion depends on one decision. Deleting inconvenient points without reporting it hides that dependence.", s: "data/outliers-in-practice", l: "Outliers: Detect, Investigate, Decide" },
      { t: "Winsorizing", d: "Capping extreme values at a chosen percentile (or the next-highest legitimate value) instead of deleting them. It keeps the row and the sample size while limiting the influence a single huge value has on the mean and SD.", s: "data/outliers-in-practice", l: "Outliers: Detect, Investigate, Decide" },
      { t: "1.5 × IQR rule", d: "Flags any value below Q1 − 1.5·IQR or above Q3 + 1.5·IQR — the fence that draws a boxplot's whiskers. Quartiles barely move with extreme values, so an outlier cannot widen the fences to hide itself, as it can by inflating the SD in a z-score rule.", s: "data/outliers-in-practice", l: "Outliers: Detect, Investigate, Decide" },
      { t: "Log transformation", d: "Replacing x with log(x) to pull in a long right tail; it turns multiplicative structure into additive and can restore normality without discarding any data. Needs positive values (use log(x+1) for zeros); the mean of the logs back-transforms to the geometric mean.", s: "data/transformations-and-recoding", l: "Transformations & Recoding" },
      { t: "Median split", d: "Chopping a continuous predictor at its median into 'high' and 'low'. It looks tidy but wastes information: it discards the variation within each half, shrinking a correlation to roughly 0.80 of its value. That costs about as much power as throwing away a third of your data. Keep variables continuous.", s: "data/transformations-and-recoding", l: "Transformations & Recoding" },
      { t: "Reverse coding", d: "Flipping a negatively worded item so it points the same way as the rest of its scale. On a 1 to 5 scale the reversed score is 6 − x, and on a 1 to k scale it is (k + 1) − x. Do it before the items are combined or analyzed. A skipped item correlates negatively with the rest of its scale. In a composite it cancels out part of the other items and lowers the reliability, and in a factor analysis it can produce a second factor made entirely of the reversed items.", s: "data/transformations-and-recoding", l: "Transformations & Recoding" },
      { t: "Composite score", d: "A single score per person formed by averaging (or summing) several items that measure one construct, once every item is reverse-coded to point the same way. When an item is missing, a mean of the answered items stays on the item scale and a sum does not.", s: "data/transformations-and-recoding", l: "Transformations & Recoding" },
      { t: "Pivoting (reshaping)", d: "Moving a dataset between wide (one row per subject, timepoints as columns) and long (one row per measurement) form. pivot_longer/melt turns column headers into a key column of values; pivot_wider/pivot spreads them back. Lossless and reversible when each row is uniquely keyed.", s: "data/wide-vs-long-data", l: "Wide vs Long Data" },
      { t: "Join (merge)", d: "Combining two tables by matching rows on a shared key, usually a participant ID, never by row order. The join type determines what happens to keys present in only one table.", s: "data/merging-datasets", l: "Merging Datasets" },
      { t: "Inner vs left join", d: "An inner join keeps only keys present in both tables. A left join keeps every row of the main table and fills NA where the other table has no match, so no one is dropped. Use a left join by default and an inner join only when you want complete cases.", s: "data/merging-datasets", l: "Merging Datasets" },
      { t: "Many-to-many join", d: "What happens when the join key repeats in both tables: every copy matches every copy, multiplying rows. It happens without any error message, so a clean 200-person dataset can become 380 rows. Check key uniqueness and the before/after row count.", s: "data/merging-datasets", l: "Merging Datasets" },
      { t: "Reproducible workflow", d: "An analysis captured as scripts that turn immutable raw data into every reported number the same way on any machine. It can be rerun, corrected, and audited, which an unrecorded point-and-click session cannot. The test is whether a stranger could rerun it from your files alone.", s: "data/reproducible-workflows", l: "Reproducible Workflows" },
      { t: "Random seed", d: "A fixed starting value for the random-number generator (set.seed(1), default_rng(1)) that makes every bootstrap, simulation, shuffle, or random split come out identical on every run. Unseeded randomness is a classic source of irreproducibility.", s: "data/reproducible-workflows", l: "Reproducible Workflows" },
      { t: "De-identification", d: "Removing direct identifiers (name, email, address) from a dataset. Weaker than anonymization: quasi-identifiers like age, sex, and ZIP can still re-identify people in combination (Sweeney found ~87% of Americans unique on ZIP + birth date + sex).", s: "data/data-privacy-basics", l: "Data Privacy Basics" },
      { t: "Quasi-identifier", d: "A field that doesn't identify someone alone but narrows the field fast in combination with others: age, sex, ZIP, job, dates. Re-identification works by combining them, and k-anonymity protects people by coarsening them.", s: "data/data-privacy-basics", l: "Data Privacy Basics" },
      { t: "k-anonymity", d: "A privacy standard: a dataset is k-anonymous if every person shares their quasi-identifier combination with at least k − 1 others, so no record can be narrowed to a group smaller than k. Higher k is safer; raise it by generalizing (age → decade) or suppressing fields.", s: "data/data-privacy-basics", l: "Data Privacy Basics" },
      { t: "Pseudonymization", d: "Replacing direct identifiers with a code while a separate, secured key still links codes to people. Reversible by design (useful for follow-ups), so the data are not anonymous while the key exists. Store the key apart from the data with tight access.", s: "data/data-privacy-basics", l: "Data Privacy Basics" },
      { t: "Belmont Report", d: "The 1979 U.S. report, written after Tuskegee, that distilled research ethics into three principles — respect for persons, beneficence, and justice — each mapped to a practical requirement (informed consent, risk–benefit assessment, fair participant selection). Ethics committees still work from its framework.", s: "ethics/why-research-ethics", l: "Why Research Ethics Exists" },
      { t: "Beneficence", d: "The Belmont principle of doing no harm while maximizing likely benefits and minimizing possible harms. Every ethics review weighs risks against benefits on this principle.", s: "ethics/why-research-ethics", l: "Why Research Ethics Exists" },
      { t: "Respect for persons", d: "The Belmont principle that people are autonomous agents who decide for themselves, with extra protection for those of diminished autonomy. In practice it becomes informed consent.", s: "ethics/why-research-ethics", l: "Why Research Ethics Exists" },
      { t: "Informed consent", d: "A participant's agreement to take part, valid only when all three hold: they are adequately informed, they actually comprehend it, and they choose voluntarily (free to refuse or withdraw). A signature missing any one is not valid consent.", s: "ethics/informed-consent-and-irb", l: "Informed Consent & Ethics Committees" },
      { t: "Assent", d: "A minor's own age-appropriate agreement to take part, required alongside a guardian's consent. A parent's yes does not override a child's refusal; the same logic covers adults with diminished capacity.", s: "ethics/informed-consent-and-irb", l: "Informed Consent & Ethics Committees" },
      { t: "Institutional Review Board (IRB)", d: "The committee (a Research Ethics Committee outside the U.S.) that reviews a study before data collection, checking that risks are justified, consent is valid, recruitment isn't coercive, and data are protected. Review is scaled by risk: exempt, expedited, or full-board.", s: "ethics/informed-consent-and-irb", l: "Informed Consent & Ethics Committees" },
      { t: "Deception (in research)", d: "Withholding or misrepresenting information to participants. Permissible only when there is no reasonable alternative, harm is minimal, and a full debriefing follows. It also erodes trust, and suspicious participants bring back demand characteristics.", s: "ethics/deception-and-debriefing", l: "Deception & Debriefing" },
      { t: "Debriefing", d: "The post-study session that follows deception. It reveals and explains what was misrepresented, corrects false beliefs, offers the right to withdraw one's data, and checks on wellbeing, so participants leave no worse off than they arrived.", s: "ethics/deception-and-debriefing", l: "Deception & Debriefing" },
      { t: "Confidentiality", d: "The promise to protect identifiable data and never disclose an individual's responses. Distinct from anonymity: confidential data can still be traced to a person, so you hold the link and guard it. The promise is strong but not absolute: a credible risk of serious harm can override it, which should be flagged in the consent form.", s: "ethics/privacy-and-confidentiality", l: "Privacy & Confidentiality" },
      { t: "Data minimization", d: "The data-protection principle of collecting only the variables your research question actually needs. Every extra field has to be protected and makes re-identification easier.", s: "ethics/privacy-and-confidentiality", l: "Privacy & Confidentiality" },
      { t: "Questionable research practices (QRPs)", d: "The gray-zone practices between honest flexibility and fraud that inflate false positives using real data: optional stopping, selective outcome reporting, HARKing, p-hacking. Researchers rarely see them as cheating, surveys find them widespread, and they are the main cause of the replication crisis.", s: "ethics/questionable-research-practices", l: "Questionable Research Practices" },
      { t: "Optional stopping", d: "Repeatedly testing as data arrive and stopping the moment p < .05 (or adding 'just a few more' participants when it isn't). Each extra look is another chance to cross the threshold, so it turns a nominal 5% false-positive rate into 20% or more. Planned interim analyses with corrected thresholds are the legitimate alternative.", s: "ethics/questionable-research-practices", l: "Questionable Research Practices" },
      { t: "Plagiarism", d: "Presenting someone's words, ideas, or work as your own. Forms include verbatim copying, mosaic/patchwriting (synonym-swapping while keeping structure), self-plagiarism (reusing your own submitted work as new), and idea theft. A citation covers the idea; quotation marks cover the exact words, and you need both.", s: "ethics/plagiarism-authorship-and-citation", l: "Plagiarism, Authorship & Citation" },
      { t: "Self-plagiarism", d: "Reusing your own previously submitted or published work as though it were new, such as recycling a graded essay into a thesis without disclosure. Fixed by disclosing, citing the earlier work, and reworking it for the new context, not by swapping a few words.", s: "ethics/plagiarism-authorship-and-citation", l: "Plagiarism, Authorship & Citation" },
      { t: "Authorship (ICMJE criteria)", d: "Authorship requires all four: a substantial contribution, drafting or critically revising the work, approving the final version, and accountability for it. Credit comes with responsibility, so authorship can't be given as a favor. People who helped but don't meet all four belong in the acknowledgements.", s: "ethics/plagiarism-authorship-and-citation", l: "Plagiarism, Authorship & Citation" },
      { t: "Gift and ghost authorship", d: "Two ways a byline misrepresents who did the work. Gift (honorary) authorship lists someone who didn't meet the criteria, such as a lab head or someone added as a favor. Ghost authorship omits someone who did, such as the student who ran the study. Both distort responsibility; the fix is to agree authorship and order openly and early.", s: "ethics/plagiarism-authorship-and-citation", l: "Plagiarism, Authorship & Citation" },
      { t: "Hallucination (AI)", d: "An AI language model producing fluent, confident output that is false — most notoriously citations for papers that don't exist, plus invented statistics and 'facts'. It is a built-in result of predicting plausible text, not an occasional bug, so verify every AI-supplied fact, number, and reference independently before you use it.", s: "ethics/ai-in-research-ethics", l: "Using AI Tools Ethically" },
      { t: "Fabrication and falsification", d: "The two core forms of research misconduct about the data itself: fabrication invents data or results that were never collected; falsification alters real data by changing values, deleting cases, or doctoring images. Questionable research practices are different: the data are real, but the analysis is distorted.", s: "ethics/fraud-and-self-correction", l: "Fraud & How Science Self-Corrects" },
      { t: "Retraction", d: "The formal withdrawal of a published paper from the scientific record because it can no longer be trusted, through fraud, serious error, or unreliable data. The article usually stays visible, stamped 'retracted' with a notice explaining why, but should no longer be cited as valid evidence. It is not a synonym for fraud: many retractions follow honest mistakes.", s: "ethics/fraud-and-self-correction", l: "Fraud & How Science Self-Corrects" },
      { t: "GRIM test", d: "Granularity-Related Inconsistency of Means: an arithmetic check for means of whole-number data. Such a mean is an integer total divided by n, so only fractions with denominator n can occur. A reported mean that falls between reachable values (e.g. 5.19 from 28 responses) is impossible and points to a typo or fabrication. It is informative only when n < 10^(decimals reported).", s: "ethics/fraud-and-self-correction", l: "Fraud & How Science Self-Corrects" },
      { t: "Prediction vs. inference", d: "The two goals a model can serve (Breiman's 'two cultures'). Inference/explanation estimates which variables matter and how much, with uncertainty (coefficients, CIs, p-values); prediction estimates the outcome for new cases, judged by held-out error. The same model can serve either, but each goal favors a different level of complexity.", s: "ml/prediction-vs-explanation", l: "Prediction vs Explanation" },
      { t: "Data leakage", d: "When information from the test set (or the future) gets into training, so the model is evaluated on data it has already seen. The reported score is inflated while true performance stays low. Classic causes: preprocessing before the split, the same subject in both sets, and tuning on the test set.", s: "ml/train-test-split-and-generalization", l: "Train/Test Splits & Generalization" },
      { t: "Train/test split", d: "Holding back part of the data to evaluate a model on cases it has not seen: the model learns on the training set and is scored, once, on the untouched test set. Stratify to preserve class proportions, and split by subject when one person contributes many rows, so the test set holds people the model has never seen.", s: "ml/train-test-split-and-generalization", l: "Train/Test Splits & Generalization" },
      { t: "Regularization", d: "Adding a penalty on coefficient size to the fitting objective so coefficients are deliberately shrunk toward zero. It trades a little bias for a large drop in variance, curbing overfitting when predictors are many or correlated; the penalty strength λ is chosen by cross-validation.", s: "ml/regularization-ridge-and-lasso", l: "Regularization: Ridge & Lasso" },
      { t: "Ridge regression (L2)", d: "Regularized regression that penalizes the sum of squared coefficients. It shrinks every coefficient smoothly toward zero but never exactly to zero, so all predictors stay in the model. This is especially useful when predictors are correlated, because ridge shares the coefficient among them rather than picking one.", s: "ml/regularization-ridge-and-lasso", l: "Regularization: Ridge & Lasso" },
      { t: "Lasso (L1)", d: "Regularized regression that penalizes the sum of absolute coefficients, driving weak ones to exactly zero. This performs automatic variable selection, yielding a sparse, interpretable model. Among correlated predictors it tends to keep one and zero the rest; elastic net blends lasso and ridge.", s: "ml/regularization-ridge-and-lasso", l: "Regularization: Ridge & Lasso" },
      { t: "Confusion matrix", d: "The 2×2 table of a classifier's predictions against the truth: true positives, false positives, false negatives, and true negatives. Every classification metric (accuracy, precision, recall, specificity, F1) is just a ratio of these four counts, so reading the matrix directly tells you which kind of error the model makes.", s: "ml/classification-metrics", l: "Classification Metrics & the Accuracy Trap" },
      { t: "Precision and recall", d: "Two views of a classifier's positive predictions. Recall (sensitivity, true-positive rate) = TP/(TP+FN): of the real positives, how many were caught. Precision = TP/(TP+FP): of everything flagged, how many were right. Screening prizes recall (don't miss a case); spam filtering prizes precision (don't bin a real email). F1 is their harmonic mean.", s: "ml/classification-metrics", l: "Classification Metrics & the Accuracy Trap" },
      { t: "Accuracy trap", d: "When high overall accuracy hides a useless model because one class is rare. If 2% of cases are positive, always predicting 'negative' scores 98% accuracy while catching nothing. Because accuracy weights every case equally, an imbalanced base rate lets the majority class dominate. Report recall and precision instead.", s: "ml/classification-metrics", l: "Classification Metrics & the Accuracy Trap" },
      { t: "ROC curve", d: "A plot of true-positive rate against false-positive rate as the decision threshold sweeps across every value. The closer the curve comes to the top-left corner, the better; the 45° diagonal is chance. It lets you compare classifiers without first committing to a single operating threshold.", s: "ml/roc-curves-and-auc", l: "ROC Curves & AUC" },
      { t: "AUC (area under the ROC curve)", d: "A single-number summary of a ROC curve, between 0 and 1. It equals the probability that the model scores a randomly chosen positive case above a randomly chosen negative one: 0.5 is chance, 0.9+ is excellent. Being threshold-free and base-rate-independent, it measures ranking ability rather than accuracy at one cutoff.", s: "ml/roc-curves-and-auc", l: "ROC Curves & AUC" },
      { t: "Decision tree", d: "A model that classifies (or predicts) by a sequence of yes/no questions on one feature at a time. Each split is chosen to reduce Gini impurity, so the data divide into ever-purer groups and the feature space into labeled rectangles. A tree reads like a flowchart, but a single tree is unstable, and deeper trees overfit more.", s: "ml/decision-trees", l: "Decision Trees" },
      { t: "Gini impurity", d: "A measure of how mixed a group's class labels are: the chance you'd mislabel a random member if you guessed in proportion to the group's mix. It is 0 for a perfectly pure group and 0.5 for a 50/50 two-class group. Decision trees pick each split to reduce the size-weighted Gini impurity of the resulting groups the most.", s: "ml/decision-trees", l: "Decision Trees" },
      { t: "Random forest", d: "An ensemble that averages the votes of many decision trees, each grown on a bootstrap sample of the data using a random subset of features at every split. Bagging plus feature randomness makes the trees disagree, so averaging cancels much of their individual noise. The large drop in variance makes a forest one of the strongest predictors that needs little tuning, at the cost of interpretability.", s: "ml/random-forests-and-ensembles", l: "Random Forests & Ensembles" },
      { t: "Bagging (bootstrap aggregating)", d: "Training each model in an ensemble on its own bootstrap sample (n rows drawn with replacement) and averaging their predictions. Because the samples differ, the models make partly independent errors that cancel on averaging, reducing variance. It is the core idea behind random forests.", s: "ml/random-forests-and-ensembles", l: "Random Forests & Ensembles" },
      { t: "Out-of-bag (OOB) error", d: "A free validation estimate from bagging: each bootstrap sample omits about 37% of rows, so every row can be predicted using only the trees that never saw it. Comparing those predictions to the truth approximates cross-validated test error without a separate holdout set.", s: "ml/random-forests-and-ensembles", l: "Random Forests & Ensembles" },
      { t: "Variable importance", d: "A ranking of how much each predictor helped an ensemble predict, via impurity reduction across splits or, more reliably, permutation (shuffle a column and measure the accuracy drop). Useful but biased toward high-cardinality predictors and distorted by correlation. It measures predictive help, not causation.", s: "ml/random-forests-and-ensembles", l: "Random Forests & Ensembles" },
      { t: "k-nearest neighbors (k-NN)", d: "A nonparametric method that classifies a new point by the majority vote of its k closest training points (or averages them, for regression). It keeps the training data and fits no parameters. Larger k gives smoother decision boundaries, features must be scaled because distance depends on scale, and it works poorly in high dimensions.", s: "ml/knn-and-distance", l: "k-NN & Why Distance Gets Weird" },
      { t: "Curse of dimensionality", d: "The ways low-dimensional intuition breaks as features multiply: volume explodes, data becomes sparse, and the distances between points become nearly equal, so 'nearest' loses meaning and distance-based methods fail. The ball inside a unit cube fills ~52% of it in 3-D but ~0.25% in 10-D. Fixes: dimensionality reduction and feature selection.", s: "ml/knn-and-distance", l: "k-NN & Why Distance Gets Weird" },
      { t: "k-means clustering", d: "An unsupervised algorithm that partitions unlabeled data into k clusters by alternating two steps until stable: assign each point to the nearest centroid, then move each centroid to its cluster's mean. Fast and useful, but it assumes convex, roughly spherical clusters, needs you to choose k, and is sensitive to initialization.", s: "ml/clustering-kmeans", l: "Clustering & k-Means" },
      { t: "Elbow method", d: "A heuristic for choosing k in k-means: plot the within-cluster sum of squares against k and look for the 'elbow' where the curve stops dropping steeply. Beyond that bend, each extra cluster lowers the sum of squares only a little, so the elbow marks a k that captures the structure without over-splitting. The bend is often ambiguous, so pair it with the silhouette and theory.", s: "ml/clustering-kmeans", l: "Clustering & k-Means" },
      { t: "Dimensionality reduction", d: "Compressing data with many variables into a few new ones you can plot or model, while preserving as much structure as possible. Linear methods like PCA project onto directions of maximum variance and keep global distances roughly intact. Nonlinear methods like t-SNE and UMAP show local structure clearly but distort global geometry. It eases the curse of dimensionality and lets you plot high-dimensional data.", s: "ml/dimensionality-reduction", l: "Dimensionality Reduction" },
      { t: "t-SNE / UMAP", d: "Nonlinear dimensionality-reduction methods that build a 2-D layout preserving each point's local neighborhood, so clusters are easy to see. They are excellent for SEEING structure but not for measuring it. Cluster sizes, between-cluster distances, and even the number of clumps change with the perplexity (or n_neighbors) setting and the random seed, so never run statistics on the coordinates.", s: "ml/dimensionality-reduction", l: "Dimensionality Reduction" },
      { t: "Artificial neuron", d: "The building block of a neural network: it takes a weighted sum of its inputs plus a bias and passes the total through an activation (squashing) function. With a sigmoid activation this is exactly logistic regression, and one neuron draws a single straight decision boundary. Networks get their flexibility from connecting many neurons in layers.", s: "ml/neural-networks-intuition", l: "Neural Networks: The Intuition" },
      { t: "Gradient descent", d: "The standard training algorithm: picture the loss as a landscape over the model's weights, read the local downhill slope (the gradient), and take a small step that way, repeating until the loss flattens. The step size is the learning rate: too small and it crawls, too large and it overshoots and diverges. Backpropagation is the method that computes the gradients efficiently through a network's layers.", s: "ml/neural-networks-intuition", l: "Neural Networks: The Intuition" },
      { t: "Large language model (LLM)", d: "A neural network trained to predict the next token (word-piece) over a vast corpus; generating text is just doing that repeatedly. This makes it fluent and useful, but it stores patterns rather than facts and is trained for plausibility, not truth. Fabricated facts and citations (hallucinations) are therefore built in, and every output must be verified. It cannot be an author because it cannot be accountable.", s: "ml/llms-and-ai-in-research", l: "LLMs & AI in Your Research Workflow" },
      { t: "IMRaD", d: "The standard structure of an empirical paper — Introduction, Methods, Results, and Discussion — with one job each: why the study, what you did, what you found, and what it means. It follows an 'hourglass' shape: broad context narrowing to a hypothesis, the specific study in the middle, then widening back out to implications.", s: "writing/imrad-structure", l: "The IMRaD Structure" },
      { t: "APA style", d: "The American Psychological Association's rules for formatting and reporting, ubiquitous in the social sciences. For statistics, italicize Latin symbols (t, F, r, M, SD, d, N) but not Greek (α, β, χ², η²). Drop leading zeros only on numbers bounded by 1 (p, r). Floor tiny p-values at p < .001, never write p = .000, and report df in parentheses.", s: "writing/reporting-statistics-apa", l: "Reporting Statistics in APA Style" },
      { t: "Truncated axis", d: "A chart whose value axis starts somewhere other than zero, so bar lengths (or gaps between lines) are no longer proportional to the numbers. It exaggerates small differences while every plotted value stays technically correct, and it is the most common way a chart oversells a result. Bar charts should almost always start at 0.", s: "writing/tables-and-figures", l: "Tables & Figures That Don't Lie" },
      { t: "Chartjunk", d: "Ink that adds no information — fake 3-D, gradients, drop shadows, heavy gridlines, decorative rainbow palettes. Coined by Edward Tufte, it clutters a figure and can actively mislead (3-D distorts which bar looks taller). The fix is restraint: one flat color, minimal gridlines, and no mark that does not show data.", s: "writing/tables-and-figures", l: "Tables & Figures That Don't Lie" },
      { t: "Results section", d: "The part of a paper that reports what the analyses found, the statistics in plain past-tense sentences, and nothing about what they mean. Each analysis is one sentence: what was tested, the descriptives, the test statistic with its df and exact p, and the effect size. Interpretation is deferred to the Discussion.", s: "writing/writing-results", l: "From Output to Results Section" },
      { t: "Marginal significance", d: "The discouraged habit of describing a non-significant result, usually p between .05 and .10, as 'approaching' or 'a trend toward' significance. Significance is a threshold set in advance, so p = .06 simply didn't cross it. Report the exact p-value and the effect size with its confidence interval, and interpret those.", s: "writing/nonsignificant-results", l: "Writing About Non-Significant Results" },
      { t: "Absence of evidence", d: "The principle that a non-significant result (p > .05) is not proof that an effect is zero. It may just mean the study was too small to detect a real effect. 'Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.' Only a narrow confidence interval around zero lets you argue that an effect is negligible.", s: "writing/nonsignificant-results", l: "Writing About Non-Significant Results" },
      { t: "Equivalence testing", d: "An approach that flips the usual question from 'is the effect exactly zero?' to 'can we rule out effects large enough to matter?'. A non-significant result with a narrow confidence interval close to zero supports practical equivalence; a wide interval does not. It is the rigorous way to argue for 'no meaningful difference.'", s: "writing/nonsignificant-results", l: "Writing About Non-Significant Results" },
      { t: "Calibration (of claims)", d: "Matching the strength of a written claim to the strength of the evidence. The verb should fit the design (correlational data support 'was associated with,' not 'causes'). The adjective should fit the effect size ('modest,' not 'strong,' for r = .28), and the scope should fit the sample (a group average is not 'every' person). Over- and under-claiming both break calibration.", s: "writing/discussion-and-limitations", l: "Discussion & Limitations" },
      { t: "Abstract", d: "The roughly 150–250-word summary that opens a paper, and the part most readers actually read. A complete abstract makes five moves: context (the gap), aim (the question), method (who and how), a result stated with numbers, and a calibrated conclusion. Write it last, once the final results and conclusion are known, and give the paper a findable, keyword-rich title.", s: "writing/abstracts-and-titles", l: "Abstracts & Titles" },
      { t: "Internal consistency (of a paper)", d: "Whether a paper agrees with itself: the n in the abstract matches the method, the degrees of freedom fit the reported n and design, and each statistic reads the same in every section. Every table, figure, and citation should also be accounted for. Graders check it first because a contradiction is quick to spot and casts doubt on everything else.", s: "writing/final-checklist", l: "The Final Checklist" }
];
